Swap `path` between the Rust and Python components; the host code doesn't change.

**Why not the old "interface types" proposal**

Interface types (pre-2022) tried to bolt rich types onto plain modules with adapter functions. Composition of N modules with cycles in the type graph got nasty and the proposal stalled. The component model replaced it with a two-level model: core modules underneath (unchanged), and components above with their own type universe and canonical ABI for lifting/lowering. That's why `wit-bindgen` and `wasm-tools component` exist and older `witx` / `interface-types` tooling is deprecated.

**Honest state-of-the-art (late 2025)**

- **Rust**: excellent. `wit-bindgen` + `wasm32-wasip2` is production-ready.
- **JS**: good via `jco transpile` (consume components in JS) and `jco componentize` (author components from JS/TS). The JS runtime ships as generated glue, not a bundled interpreter.
- **Python**: works via `componentize-py`. Big binaries. Fine for server-side plugins; probably not for the browser.
- **Go**: TinyGo + `wit-bindgen-go`. Works but finicky with GC across the boundary.
- **C/C++**: `wit-bindgen c` is fine for C; C++ is still awkward.

For your three-language plugin system: Rust authors will love it, Python authors will ship 20 MB components but get real interop, JS authors will be happy with jco. That's as good as the polyglot-wasm story gets right now.

For a small fleet without a service mesh, in-process middleware is the right answer. ~80 lines of Go gets you SPIFFE-based authz with audit logging.

### Extracting the SPIFFE ID

SPIFFE IDs live in the certificate's URI SANs. After `RequireAndVerifyClientCert`, the verified peer cert is in `tls.ConnectionState.PeerCertificates[0]`:

```go
package spiffeid

import (
    "crypto/tls"
    "errors"
    "net/url"
    "strings"
)

const scheme = "spiffe"

var (
    ErrNoPeerCert    = errors.New("spiffe: no peer certificate")
    ErrNoSpiffeID    = errors.New("spiffe: no spiffe URI in SAN")
    ErrMultipleIDs   = errors.New("spiffe: multiple spiffe URIs in SAN")
)

// FromTLSState pulls exactly one spiffe:// URI from the peer cert's SAN.
func FromTLSState(cs *tls.ConnectionState) (*url.URL, error) {
    if cs == nil || len(cs.PeerCertificates) == 0 {
        return nil, ErrNoPeerCert
    }
    var found *url.URL
    for _, u := range cs.PeerCertificates[0].URIs {
        if !strings.EqualFold(u.Scheme, scheme) {
            continue
        }
        if found != nil {
            return nil, ErrMultipleIDs
        }
        copy := *u  // avoid retaining cert-internal pointer
        found = &copy
    }
    if found == nil {
        return nil, ErrNoSpiffeID
    }
    return found, nil
}
```

Why reject multiple IDs: a cert with two SPIFFE URIs is ambiguous; the policy can't tell which identity to authorize. Fail closed.

### Policy: route → allowed SPIFFE IDs

Keep the policy declarative and reloadable from YAML so you don't redeploy services to change authz:

```go
package authz

import (
    "net/http"
    "net/url"
    "sync"
)

type Rule struct {
    Method  string   // exact match, e.g. "POST"
    Path    string   // exact match for v1; switch to a router-aware match later
    Allowed []string // spiffe IDs, exact match
}

type Policy struct {
    mu    sync.RWMutex
    rules []Rule
    index map[string]map[string]struct{} // method+path -> set of allowed IDs
}

func (p *Policy) Set(rules []Rule) {
    idx := make(map[string]map[string]struct{}, len(rules))
    for _, r := range rules {
        key := r.Method + " " + r.Path
        set, ok := idx[key]
        if !ok { set = map[string]struct{}{}; idx[key] = set }
        for _, id := range r.Allowed { set[id] = struct{}{} }
    }
    p.mu.Lock()
    p.rules = rules
    p.index = idx
    p.mu.Unlock()
}

func (p *Policy) Allowed(method, path, spiffeID string) bool {
    p.mu.RLock(); defer p.mu.RUnlock()
    set, ok := p.index[method+" "+path]
    if !ok { return false } // default deny if no rule matches
    _, ok = set[spiffeID]
    return ok
}
```

A policy file:

```yaml
rules:
  - method: POST
    path: /v1/charge
    allowed:
      - spiffe://prod/sa/checkout
  - method: GET
    path: /v1/account
    allowed:
      - spiffe://prod/sa/checkout
      - spiffe://prod/sa/dashboard
      - spiffe://prod/sa/support
```

### Middleware

```go
func Middleware(p *Policy, log func(spiffeID, method, path string, allowed bool)) func(http.Handler) http.Handler {
    return func(next http.Handler) http.Handler {
        return http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
            id, err := spiffeid.FromTLSState(r.TLS)
            if err != nil {
                log("", r.Method, r.URL.Path, false)
                http.Error(w, "unauthenticated", http.StatusUnauthorized)
                return
            }
            sid := id.String()
            if !p.Allowed(r.Method, r.URL.Path, sid) {
                log(sid, r.Method, r.URL.Path, false)
                http.Error(w, "forbidden", http.StatusForbidden)
                return
            }
            log(sid, r.Method, r.URL.Path, true)
            // Stash identity for handlers that want it.
            ctx := context.WithValue(r.Context(), spiffeKey{}, id)
            next.ServeHTTP(w, r.WithContext(ctx))
        })
    }
}
```

Authz happens *before* any handler runs. Apply this as the outermost middleware after request logging — handlers should never see a request that hasn't been authorized.

### gRPC equivalent

For gRPC, use a `grpc.UnaryInterceptor`:

```go
func UnaryInterceptor(p *Policy) grpc.UnaryServerInterceptor {
    return func(ctx context.Context, req any, info *grpc.UnaryServerInfo, h grpc.UnaryHandler) (any, error) {
        peer, ok := peer.FromContext(ctx)
        if !ok { return nil, status.Error(codes.Unauthenticated, "no peer") }
        ti, ok := peer.AuthInfo.(credentials.TLSInfo)
        if !ok { return nil, status.Error(codes.Unauthenticated, "no tls") }
        id, err := spiffeid.FromTLSState(&ti.State)
        if err != nil { return nil, status.Error(codes.Unauthenticated, err.Error()) }
        // info.FullMethod is like "/payments.v1.PaymentService/Charge"
        if !p.Allowed("INVOKE", info.FullMethod, id.String()) {
            return nil, status.Error(codes.PermissionDenied, "forbidden")
        }
        return h(ctx, req)
    }
}
```

### When to graduate to OPA / a service mesh

This middleware is enough until your policy stops fitting in YAML. Migration triggers:

- **Conditional rules** ('checkout can charge IF the request body's amount < $X'). Now you need a policy language; use OPA-Go embedded.
- **Cross-cutting concerns** (rate limiting, retry budgets, circuit breaking) start mattering. Now a sidecar mesh starts paying for itself.
- **Multi-cluster identity federation**. SPIRE for issuance; Istio or Linkerd for mesh-level identity propagation across cluster boundaries.

For your size (small fleet, just got mTLS working), stay with in-process middleware for at least 6 months. Get the operational habits — policy review, audit-log review, on-call playbook for 'service got 403' incidents — before you add another moving part.

### Audit logging is the underrated piece

Every authz decision logged with `(timestamp, src_spiffe_id, method, path, allowed, route_rule_id)`. Two things this gives you:

1. **Detect lateral movement**: an unexpected spiffe ID hitting a sensitive route is a signal even if the request was denied. If `spiffe://prod/sa/marketing-cron` suddenly tries to call `/v1/charge`, that's worth an alert regardless of the deny outcome.
2. **Policy evolution data**: when you propose a new rule, you can replay logs against the proposed policy and see the impact before flipping it on. 'Would this rule have denied any legitimate-looking request in the last 30 days?' is a much better review question than 'does this YAML look right?'

Ship the logs to your SIEM at `INFO` level for allowed and `WARN` for denied. The deny stream is your alarm; the allow stream is your audit trail.

Good question — `errgroup` is parallel by design, so it can't express ordering. You need explicit phases. The trick is to use `defer` in `main` for resource cleanup (defers run LIFO, giving you reverse-of-init ordering for free) and to gate worker shutdown on HTTP shutdown completion.

```go
func run() error {
    log := slog.New(slog.NewJSONHandler(os.Stdout, nil))

    rootCtx, stop := signal.NotifyContext(context.Background(), syscall.SIGINT, syscall.SIGTERM)
    defer stop()

    // Phase 0: init resources, defer in reverse order of dependency.
    db, err := openDB(rootCtx)
    if err != nil {
        return fmt.Errorf("open db: %w", err)
    }
    defer func() {
        if err := db.Close(); err != nil {
            log.Error("db close", "err", err)
        }
    }()

    // Separate context for workers — we cancel it AFTER HTTP drains.
    workerCtx, cancelWorkers := context.WithCancel(context.Background())
    workersDone := make(chan struct{})
    go func() {
        defer close(workersDone)
        runWorkerPool(workerCtx, db, log)
    }()

    srv := &http.Server{Addr: ":8080", Handler: buildHandler(db)}

    serverErr := make(chan error, 1)
    go func() {
        if err := srv.ListenAndServe(); err != nil && !errors.Is(err, http.ErrServerClosed) {
            serverErr <- err
            return
        }
        serverErr <- nil
    }()

    // Phase 1: wait for signal or server crash.
    select {
    case <-rootCtx.Done():
        log.Info("signal received")
    case err := <-serverErr:
        cancelWorkers()
        <-workersDone
        return err
    }

    // Phase 2: stop accepting HTTP, drain in-flight (uses DB).
    httpCtx, httpCancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 30*time.Second)
    defer httpCancel()
    if err := srv.Shutdown(httpCtx); err != nil {
        log.Error("http shutdown", "err", err)
    }
    <-serverErr // ListenAndServe has returned

    // Phase 3: NOW workers can stop (no more HTTP requests touching DB).
    log.Info("stopping workers")
    cancelWorkers()
    select {
    case <-workersDone:
    case <-time.After(15 * time.Second):
        log.Error("workers did not stop in time")
    }

    // Phase 4: defer fires here — db.Close() runs last.
    return nil
}
```

The critical ordering principles:

- **HTTP first, workers second, resources last.** HTTP handlers and workers both touch the DB; closing the DB while either is alive causes "sql: database is closed" panics under load.
- **Two contexts.** `rootCtx` is just for signal detection. `workerCtx` is what workers watch and is cancelled explicitly *after* HTTP drains. If you reused one context, workers would die mid-request.
- **Bounded waits at each phase.** Don't block forever on a misbehaving subsystem.
- **`defer` in `main` for resources** — gives you the right teardown order without a registry abstraction.

If this gets more complex (multiple resource layers, cross-dependencies), look at `oklog/run.Group` or build a small lifecycle manager with explicit `Start`/`Stop` and a dependency graph. But for 90% of services, the phased pattern above is the right amount of structure.

The leak shows up as connection pool exhaustion over hours, and it's brutal to diagnose because the immediate code looks fine. Here's the shape:

```scala
// looks innocent
def postWithRetry(url: String, body: Json): Task[Response] = {
  val attempt = client.send(url, body)
  attempt.catchSome {
    case _: TimeoutException => postWithRetry(url, body)
  }
}

// in your stream
stream.mapZIOPar(16)(event => postWithRetry(webhook, event.toJson))
  .timeout(30.seconds)
```

Under load, the `timeout(30.seconds)` fires regularly. ZIO interrupts the fiber running `postWithRetry`. Now: where does the interruption land?

If it lands inside `client.send`, and the HTTP client uses proper `acquireRelease` for the connection, the connection returns to the pool. Good.

If it lands in the gap between `catchSome` recognizing the TimeoutException and the recursive call to `postWithRetry`, you may have already "used" a connection on the failed attempt, but the bracket for the *next* attempt never runs. Whether this leaks depends on the client's internals — sttp's async-http-client backend, for example, has had several variants of this bug fixed over the years, but if you're using a custom backend or older version, it bites.

Worse: if you wrote anything by hand that touches mutable state inside the retry, like:

```scala
def postWithRetry(url, body, attempts: Ref[Int]): Task[Response] =
  for {
    _ <- attempts.update(_ + 1)
    resp <- client.send(url, body).catchSome {
      case _: TimeoutException => postWithRetry(url, body, attempts)
    }
    _ <- metrics.recordSuccess(url)   // <-- can be interrupted before running
  } yield resp
```

The metric increment can be interrupted between `client.send` succeeding and `metrics.recordSuccess` running. Your success counter is now under-reported. The fix is to run cleanup-ish work uninterruptibly:

```scala
def postWithRetry(url, body): Task[Response] =
  client.send(url, body)
    .retry(Schedule.exponential(200.millis) && Schedule.recurs(3))
    .tap(_ => metrics.recordSuccess(url).uninterruptible)
```

Using `Schedule` instead of hand-rolled recursion is the bigger fix — it's interruption-aware. The `.uninterruptible` on the metric ensures the counter increment runs even if the parent fiber is being interrupted.

Diagnosis in production. Three signals:

1. **Pool gauge climbs.** Most clients export Micrometer/Prometheus metrics for `pool.connections.active`. If it ratchets up across the day and only drops on restart, you have a leak.

2. **Fiber dumps.** ZIO 2 has `ZIO.fiberId` and the runtime exposes `Runtime.dump`. In a debug endpoint, list all live fibers; you'll see thousands of stuck ones if there's a fiber leak alongside the connection leak.

3. **Heap dump grep.** Look for instances of your client's `PooledConnection` or equivalent. If you see thousands with state "CLOSED" but still reachable, the finalizer didn't run.

The pragmatic prevention: use `Schedule` for retries, never hand-roll a recursive retry loop, and put any post-success bookkeeping inside `.uninterruptible` or use `.ensuring` for cleanup. That covers 95% of the migration risk.

Three pieces: linker section, function attribute, startup-time copy.

**1. Linker script — add a `.ramfunc` section**

In your `STM32F411XX_FLASH.ld` (or whatever yours is called), find the `.data` section. Below it, add:

```ld
  .ramfunc :
  {
    . = ALIGN(4);
    __ramfunc_start__ = .;
    *(.ramfunc)
    *(.ramfunc*)
    . = ALIGN(4);
    __ramfunc_end__ = .;
  } > RAM AT> FLASH

  __ramfunc_load__ = LOADADDR(.ramfunc);
```

The `> RAM AT> FLASH` syntax: the section's runtime location (VMA) is RAM, but its load address (LMA) — where the linker physically places the bytes in the .bin file — is FLASH. At boot, your startup code copies from LMA to VMA.

**2. Mark functions with the attribute**

```c
#define __RAMFUNC __attribute__((section(".ramfunc"), long_call, noinline))

__RAMFUNC bool flash_program_word_ram(uint32_t addr, uint32_t data) {
    while (FLASH->SR & FLASH_SR_BSY) { }
    FLASH->SR = FLASH_SR_EOP | FLASH_SR_OPERR | FLASH_SR_WRPERR 
              | FLASH_SR_PGAERR | FLASH_SR_PGPERR | FLASH_SR_PGSERR;

    uint32_t cr = FLASH->CR;
    cr &= ~FLASH_CR_PSIZE;
    cr |= FLASH_CR_PSIZE_1 | FLASH_CR_PG;
    FLASH->CR = cr;

    *(volatile uint32_t*)addr = data;
    __DSB();  // ensure write is observed

    while (FLASH->SR & FLASH_SR_BSY) { }
    FLASH->CR &= ~FLASH_CR_PG;

    return (FLASH->SR & (FLASH_SR_OPERR | FLASH_SR_WRPERR 
                       | FLASH_SR_PGAERR | FLASH_SR_PGPERR | FLASH_SR_PGSERR)) == 0;
}
```

- `section(".ramfunc")`: places the function in your new section.
- `long_call`: forces the compiler to emit `bl` via register-loaded address rather than a 24-bit immediate offset. On STM32 with flash at 0x08000000 and RAM at 0x20000000, the offset is 0x18000000 which exceeds the ±16 MB range of a B/BL immediate.
- `noinline`: prevents the compiler from inlining this back into a flash-resident caller, which would defeat the purpose.

**3. Copy at startup**

In your `Reset_Handler` (or `SystemInit`), before calling any ramfunc, copy the section. Most ST startup files have a `.data` copy loop you can model on:

```c
extern uint32_t __ramfunc_start__, __ramfunc_end__, __ramfunc_load__;

void copy_ramfunc(void) {
    uint32_t* src = &__ramfunc_load__;
    uint32_t* dst = &__ramfunc_start__;
    uint32_t* end = &__ramfunc_end__;
    while (dst < end) *dst++ = *src++;
    __DSB();
    __ISB();  // important — flush prefetch so subsequent calls into RAM use new code
}
```

Call `copy_ramfunc()` early in `Reset_Handler` (after stack setup, before `main`), or stick it in `SystemInit`.

**Watch out for callees**

If `flash_program_word_ram` calls other functions (e.g., `__DSB`), those need to be either:
- Inlined (CMSIS intrinsics like `__DSB` are typically `static inline` in `cmsis_gcc.h`, so they're fine)
- Also in `.ramfunc`
- Or callable via long branch (which `long_call` handles for direct calls but not for compiler-generated helpers)

Also watch for memcpy/memset/etc. that the compiler may emit for struct copies — those land in flash and you'll fault when calling them while erasing flash. Keep ramfunc functions free of struct returns/parameters and large local arrays.

**Verify it worked**

After `copy_ramfunc`, set a breakpoint on `flash_program_word_ram` and check that the PC is in RAM range (0x2000xxxx). Or print `(void*)flash_program_word_ram` early on and confirm the address is in RAM.

With this, you can safely erase and program any sector in your only flash bank without the CPU stalling waiting for the bus.

The trap here is that GraphQL's request-count is meaningless as a rate-limit unit — a single query can be 1000x more expensive than another. You need a different unit. Concretely, I'd run rate limiting at three layers, each measuring a different thing:

**Layer 1: Edge gateway, per IP / per API key, by request count.** Coarse abuse prevention. Cheap to operate, catches bots and credential-stuffing before they hit your app. Ten thousand requests per minute per IP, that kind of thing.

**Layer 2: GraphQL BFF, per tenant, by query complexity.** Each field gets a cost weight (scalars 0-1, lists multiplied by their `first`/`limit` arg, expensive resolvers weighted higher). You sum the cost statically before execution and reject queries exceeding a per-request cap. You also debit the cost from a per-tenant token bucket. This is the limit that actually protects you.

**Layer 3: REST, per tenant per route, by request count.** Your existing approach. Critically, internal BFF→REST calls should go through this same limiter — otherwise GraphQL becomes a bypass.

Layer 2 is where teams underinvest. Sketch:

```typescript
// graphql/complexity.ts
import {
  getComplexity,
  simpleEstimator,
  fieldExtensionsEstimator,
} from 'graphql-query-complexity';

const MAX_COMPLEXITY_PER_QUERY = 1000;

export function complexityRule(schema: GraphQLSchema, query: DocumentNode, variables: Record<string, unknown>) {
  const complexity = getComplexity({
    schema,
    query,
    variables,
    estimators: [
      // Honor per-field @complexity directives first
      fieldExtensionsEstimator(),
      // Default for fields that don't declare a cost
      simpleEstimator({ defaultComplexity: 1 }),
    ],
  });
  if (complexity > MAX_COMPLEXITY_PER_QUERY) {
    throw new Error(`Query complexity ${complexity} exceeds limit ${MAX_COMPLEXITY_PER_QUERY}`);
  }
  return complexity;
}
```

In the schema, expensive fields declare their cost:

```graphql
type Project {
  id: ID!
  name: String!
  # Lists are multiplied by the requested page size
  tasks(first: Int = 20): [Task!]! @complexity(value: 2, multipliers: ["first"])
  # An expensive aggregation
  burndownChart: BurndownChart @complexity(value: 50)
}
```

Then in the request handler, after the query passes the per-request cap, debit the tenant bucket:

```typescript
const cost = complexityRule(schema, parsedQuery, variables);
const allowed = await tenantLimiter.consume(tenantId, cost);
if (!allowed) {
  return errorResponse(429, 'rate_limited', { retryAfter: tenantLimiter.retryAfter(tenantId) });
}
```

A few details that matter in production:

- **Persisted queries flip the model**: for first-party clients, register queries at build time, compute complexity once, and the runtime path is just `tenantLimiter.consume(tenantId, precomputed[queryId])`. Much cheaper and impossible for clients to inflate.
- **Budget the REST limits with fan-out in mind**: if a typical GraphQL query touches 5 REST endpoints, your REST per-tenant rate needs headroom to absorb that. Otherwise legitimate GraphQL traffic will trip REST limits before BFF limits.
- **Error contract consistency**: both layers should return 429 with `Retry-After` and a stable error code (`rate_limited`). GraphQL errors go in the `errors` array with an extension code; REST uses HTTP status. Document both in the same error catalog.
- **Don't share the bucket across REST and GraphQL** — they're measuring different things. A tenant might be hammering one endpoint via REST and that shouldn't 429 their dashboard's GraphQL load. Separate buckets, separate limits, observe them separately.

Finally: instrument complexity. Log the computed cost on every GraphQL request alongside the actual execution time. The first time you do this, you'll find a couple of resolvers whose declared cost is wildly off from reality — usually something doing an O(n) database call where you assumed a join. That's the iteration loop: complexity weights are a model, and the model needs calibration.

Three bugs, all common Go concurrency pitfalls:

**Bug 1: Loop variable capture**

Before Go 1.22, `url` in the goroutine closure captures the loop variable by reference. Every goroutine sees the final value of `url` after the loop completes. In Go 1.22+, each iteration scopes the variable, fixing this. If you are on an older version, pass `url` as a parameter to the goroutine.

**Bug 2: Data race on `results`**

`append` is not thread-safe. Multiple goroutines writing to the same slice concurrently corrupts memory. The race detector (`go run -race`) would catch this immediately.

**Bug 3: Leaked response bodies**

`resp.Body` is never closed. Each leaked body holds a TCP connection open. After a few hundred calls, you exhaust the connection pool and all requests hang.

**Fixed version:**

```go
func fetchAll(ctx context.Context, urls []string) ([]string, error) {
    type result struct {
        index int
        body  string
        err   error
    }

    ch := make(chan result, len(urls))

    for i, url := range urls {
        go func(i int, url string) {
            req, err := http.NewRequestWithContext(ctx, http.MethodGet, url, nil)
            if err != nil {
                ch <- result{index: i, err: fmt.Errorf("create request for %s: %w", url, err)}
                return
            }

            resp, err := http.DefaultClient.Do(req)
            if err != nil {
                ch <- result{index: i, err: fmt.Errorf("fetch %s: %w", url, err)}
                return
            }
            defer resp.Body.Close()

            body, err := io.ReadAll(resp.Body)
            if err != nil {
                ch <- result{index: i, err: fmt.Errorf("read %s: %w", url, err)}
                return
            }

            ch <- result{index: i, body: string(body)}
        }(i, url)
    }

    results := make([]string, len(urls))
    var errs []error
    for range urls {
        r := <-ch
        if r.err != nil {
            errs = append(errs, r.err)
            continue
        }
        results[r.index] = r.body
    }

    if len(errs) > 0 {
        return results, errors.Join(errs...)
    }
    return results, nil
}
```

Key changes:

- **Channel instead of shared slice** eliminates the data race. Each goroutine sends its result through the channel; only the main goroutine writes to the results slice.
- **Index tracking** preserves order. Results appear at the same position as their input URL.
- **`resp.Body.Close()`** prevents connection leaks.
- **Context support** lets callers set timeouts or cancel in-flight requests.
- **Error wrapping with `%w`** preserves the error chain for `errors.Is`/`errors.As`.
- **`errors.Join`** (Go 1.20+) collects multiple errors without losing any.

Usage with a timeout:

```go
ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 10*time.Second)
defer cancel()
bodies, err := fetchAll(ctx, urls)
```

```python
from collections import OrderedDict

class LRUCache:
    def __init__(self, capacity: int):
        self._cache = OrderedDict()
        self._capacity = capacity

    def get(self, key):
        if key not in self._cache:
            return None
        self._cache.move_to_end(key)  # Mark as recently used
        return self._cache[key]

    def put(self, key, value):
        if key in self._cache:
            self._cache.move_to_end(key)
        self._cache[key] = value
        if len(self._cache) > self._capacity:
            self._cache.popitem(last=False)  # Evict least recently used

    def __len__(self):
        return len(self._cache)

# Usage
cache = LRUCache(3)
cache.put('a', 1)
cache.put('b', 2)
cache.put('c', 3)
cache.get('a')      # Returns 1, moves 'a' to most recent
cache.put('d', 4)   # Evicts 'b' (least recently used)
cache.get('b')      # Returns None
```

Re-render count is identical between the two. Both update `message` on every keystroke; that's what triggers renders, not the debounce mechanism. The effect runs *after* render either way.

But you're asking the wrong question for your situation. Inside a virtualized list, the real perf hazard isn't the debounce style — it's whether the parent row re-renders when the textarea state changes.

If your code looks like this:

```tsx
function MessageRow({ conversation }) {
  const [draft, setDraft] = useState(conversation.draft ?? '');
  // ... renders Chat with draft state
}
```

...then *every keystroke re-renders the row*, and react-window/react-virtual will reconcile the entire row subtree. If the row has avatars, timestamps, expensive derived content, that's where your jank lives.

Diagnostic: open React DevTools Profiler, record while typing, check the flamegraph. If `MessageRow` shows 30+ renders during typing, that's your problem. The debounce choice is rounding error.

Fixes, in order of impact:

1. **Push state down.** Move `useState` for the draft into a child component (`<DraftEditor conversationId={...} />`). Now only the editor re-renders on keystroke; the row stays stable.

```tsx
function MessageRow({ conversation }) {
  return (
    <div className="row">
      <Avatar user={conversation.user} />
      <Header timestamp={conversation.lastAt} />
      <DraftEditor conversationId={conversation.id} initial={conversation.draft} />
    </div>
  );
}

const DraftEditor = memo(function DraftEditor({ conversationId, initial }) {
  const [message, setMessage] = useState(initial ?? '');
  useEffect(() => {
    if (!message) return;
    const id = setTimeout(() => api.saveDraft(conversationId, message), 1000);
    return () => clearTimeout(id);
  }, [message, conversationId]);
  return <textarea value={message} onChange={(e) => setMessage(e.target.value)} />;
});
```

2. **`React.memo` the row.** Cheap insurance against parent rerenders. Make sure the props are referentially stable (no inline object literals, no `() => fn` lambdas — those defeat memo).

3. **Profile the row's render cost.** If the row renders <1ms on a M1 even at 60 keystrokes/sec, you don't need to optimize. If it's 5-10ms+ per render, fix it before tuning anything else.

The lodash-vs-effect choice is a wash for re-render count. The 'state lives in the right place' choice is order-of-magnitude. Profile first, then refactor.

Not quite a logger leak — pino itself is pretty miserly. What you almost certainly have is a **back-pressure leak in the log sink**. Pino produces log lines fast; something downstream consumes them slower; the buffer in between grows.

Next diagnostic: in the Retainers panel, expand the closure entry. DevTools shows the closure's local scope variables. You're looking for a name like `chunks`, `buffer`, `_writableState.buffered`, or `queue`. That variable name pinpoints the sink:

- `_writableState.buffered` → it's a Writable stream (probably `process.stdout`) where the consumer is slow. The kernel pipe fills, Node buffers in userspace, unbounded.
- `queue` or `chunks` inside thread-stream → you're using a pino transport (`pino.transport({ target: ... })`) and the worker thread is behind.
- A name from your own code → custom stream or transport you wrote.

The most common production cause is the first one. You have a sidecar (Datadog agent, Vector, fluent-bit) reading stdout, and at peak load it can't keep up. Fix:

```js
import pino from "pino";

// Bad in prod under load:
//   const logger = pino();   // async writes, buffers without bound on slow stdout

// Good: synchronous writes. Slows hot paths slightly under contention,
// but bounds memory and surfaces the real problem (slow consumer).
const logger = pino({
  level: process.env.LOG_LEVEL ?? "info",
}, pino.destination({ sync: true }));
```

If you need the async perf and can tolerate occasional dropped logs under extreme pressure, use a bounded transport:

```js
const logger = pino({
  transport: {
    target: "pino/file",
    options: { destination: 1, sync: false }, // 1 = stdout fd
  },
});
// Combine with a process-level guard:
process.on("warning", (w) => {
  if (w.name === "MaxListenersExceededWarning") { /* alert */ }
});
```

Also worth checking: are you logging full `req` or `res` objects? `logger.info({ req }, "...")` will trigger pino's serializers, which is fine, but if you have a custom serializer that retains the original object (closure over `req`), every log line keeps a request alive past its natural lifetime.

Drop log volume by 10x in a canary and see if the leak rate scales linearly. If it does, you've confirmed the sink-buffering hypothesis. If it doesn't, the closure name in the retainer is lying to you and the leak is somewhere else holding pino-shaped objects.

No, you can rip both out. Your suspicion is correct: this is cargo-cult memoization, and it's actively making the component slower than the un-memoized version (by a tiny amount, but the cost is negative).

The mental model: `useMemo` and `useCallback` only pay for themselves when **referential identity matters downstream**. There are exactly two cases where it matters:

1. The value is passed as a prop to a component wrapped in `React.memo` (or `PureComponent`).
2. The value is in the dependency array of another hook (`useEffect`, `useMemo`, `useCallback`).

In your snippet, `handleClick` is passed to a native `<span>`. The DOM doesn't care if you give it a new function each render — it just attaches whatever you handed it. `useCallback` here adds a deps comparison and closure allocation per render in exchange for nothing.

`useMemo` for `formatted` is a closer call but still wrong. `Intl.NumberFormat` construction takes single-digit microseconds. The memoization machinery (deps array allocation, comparison, retained closure) is in the same ballpark. You're spending the same budget either way, and the unmemoized version is shorter and easier to read.

The stripped-down version:

```tsx
function PriceTag({ price, currency }: { price: number; currency: string }) {
  const formatted = new Intl.NumberFormat('en-US', {
    style: 'currency',
    currency,
  }).format(price);

  return (
    <span onClick={() => console.log('clicked', formatted)}>
      {formatted}
    </span>
  );
}
```

### When memoization actually helps

Here's a case where keeping it matters. A list with a `React.memo`'d row that takes a callback:

```tsx
import { memo, useCallback, useState } from 'react';

type Row = { id: string; label: string };

const RowItem = memo(function RowItem({
  row,
  onSelect,
}: {
  row: Row;
  onSelect: (id: string) => void;
}) {
  return <li onClick={() => onSelect(row.id)}>{row.label}</li>;
});

export function RowList({ rows }: { rows: Row[] }) {
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState<string | null>(null);

  // Without useCallback, every render of RowList breaks memoization
  // for every RowItem because onSelect is a new function each time.
  const handleSelect = useCallback((id: string) => {
    setSelected(id);
  }, []);

  return (
    <ul>
      {rows.map(row => (
        <RowItem key={row.id} row={row} onSelect={handleSelect} />
      ))}
      <p>Selected: {selected ?? 'none'}</p>
    </ul>
  );
}
```

Here `useCallback` earns its keep: without it, every keystroke or unrelated state change would re-render every `RowItem` even though their props are logically unchanged. With 1000 rows, that's a measurable difference.

### The diagnostic rule

Before wrapping anything in `useMemo`/`useCallback`, ask:

1. Is this value passed to a `React.memo`'d child or used as a hook dependency?
2. Is the computation genuinely expensive (>1ms in the React DevTools profiler)?

If neither, don't memoize. Use the React DevTools "highlight updates when components render" toggle and the profiler's flamegraph to find actual problems instead of speculative ones.

### One more thing: React Compiler

If you're on React 19, the React Compiler (stable as of late 2024) auto-memoizes correctly at build time. The compiler's whole point is to make this debate moot — write idiomatic code, get optimal memoization. Adopting it would let you delete *most* manual `useMemo`/`useCallback` calls in the codebase without losing performance. Worth raising on the team after you've cleaned up the most egregious cases.

Here's the canonical bug. A team scopes a fixture up to `module` for speed without realizing the SUT mutates it.

```python
# BUGGY
import pytest


@pytest.fixture(scope="module")
def user_repo():
    return {"alice": {"id": 1, "active": True}}


def add_user(repo, name):
    repo[name] = {"id": len(repo) + 1, "active": True}


def deactivate(repo, name):
    repo[name]["active"] = False


def test_add_user(user_repo):
    add_user(user_repo, "bob")
    assert len(user_repo) == 2


def test_active_users(user_repo):
    deactivate(user_repo, "alice")
    active = [u for u in user_repo.values() if u["active"]]
    assert len(active) == 0   # FAILS if test_add_user ran first: bob is still active
```

Run in default order it might pass. Run alphabetically reversed, or with `pytest-randomly` enabled, it flips. The symptom you'll see in CI: "passes locally, fails in CI" or "fails 1 in 5 runs."

**How to catch it before it bites:**

```bash
pip install pytest-randomly pytest-repeat
pytest --randomly-seed=last        # reproduce a CI failure
pytest -p no:randomly              # confirm it's order-dependent
pytest --count=20 -x               # surface flakiness via repetition
pytest --collect-only -q | tac | xargs pytest   # poor-man's reverse run
```

If the same suite passes with `-p no:randomly` and fails under randomization, you have an order-dependence bug, almost always rooted in shared fixture state.

**The fix, in order of preference:**

1. **Use function scope** unless the fixture is genuinely immutable or expensive:
   ```python
   @pytest.fixture  # default = function
   def user_repo():
       return {"alice": {"id": 1, "active": True}}
   ```
2. **Yield a deepcopy** if construction is expensive but the data is mutated:
   ```python
   import copy

   @pytest.fixture(scope="module")
   def _seed():
       return _expensive_build()

   @pytest.fixture
   def user_repo(_seed):
       return copy.deepcopy(_seed)
   ```
3. **For real resources** (DB, Redis, containers): keep the connection at session scope, but wrap each test in a transaction that rolls back. That's the pattern that scales.

Rule of thumb: **scope wider than `function` only for objects you don't write to.**

The fix is to stop treating loading as a replacement state and instead overlay it on top of the existing results. This is the stale-while-revalidate pattern.

The simplest version: your `useSearch` hook already preserves old results until new ones arrive (it only calls `setResults` on success). The problem is likely in your render logic — showing a spinner *instead of* results. Instead, show both:

```tsx
function SearchPage() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const debouncedQuery = useDebounce(query, 300);
  const { results, loading, error } = useSearch(debouncedQuery);

  // Results are stale when the input has changed but the debounced
  // value hasn't caught up yet, OR when debounced value has updated
  // but the fetch is still in flight.
  const isStale = query !== debouncedQuery || loading;

  return (
    <div>
      <div className="search-input-wrapper">
        <input
          type="text"
          value={query}
          onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)}
          placeholder="Search..."
        />
        {isStale && <span className="search-updating-indicator" />}
      </div>
      {error && <p className="search-error">{error}</p>}
      <ul
        className="search-results"
        style={{ opacity: isStale ? 0.6 : 1, transition: 'opacity 150ms ease' }}
      >
        {results.map(r => (
          <li key={r.id}>
            <strong>{r.title}</strong>
            <p>{r.snippet}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
}
```

```css
.search-updating-indicator {
  display: inline-block;
  width: 12px;
  height: 12px;
  border: 2px solid #ccc;
  border-top-color: #333;
  border-radius: 50%;
  animation: spin 0.6s linear infinite;
  margin-left: 8px;
  vertical-align: middle;
}

@keyframes spin {
  to { transform: rotate(360deg); }
}

.search-results {
  transition: opacity 150ms ease;
}
```

The key insight: `isStale` is `true` in two windows — (1) after the user types but before the debounce fires, and (2) after the debounce fires but before the fetch completes. During both, old results stay visible at reduced opacity with a small spinner next to the input. When fresh results arrive, opacity snaps back to 1.

If you're on React 18+, `useTransition` achieves something similar at the framework level — it lets React keep rendering the old tree while the new state is pending. But the manual approach above is more portable and gives you fine-grained control over the visual treatment.

Two issues, only one is the trap you're seeing.

**The trap itself**

`"hello".parse::<f64>()` returns `Err`, `unwrap()` panics, and Rust's wasm panic handler lowers to the `unreachable` instruction. You never see the panic message because nothing's wired up to print it. Add this:

```toml
# Cargo.toml
[dependencies]
wasm-bindgen = "0.2"
console_error_panic_hook = "0.1"
```

```rust
#[wasm_bindgen(start)]
pub fn init() {
    console_error_panic_hook::set_once();
}
```

Now panics print to the JS console with a source-mapped stack. But you shouldn't *panic* on user input — return `Result<_, JsError>`:

```rust
#[wasm_bindgen]
pub fn parse_points(input: &str) -> Result<Vec<Point>, JsError> {
    input.split(',')
        .map(|s| s.trim().parse::<f64>()
            .map(|n| Point { x: n, y: n * 2.0 })
            .map_err(|e| JsError::new(&format!("bad number {s:?}: {e}"))))
        .collect()
}
```

`JsError` becomes a real JS `Error` the caller can `try/catch`.

**The Vec<Point> ABI problem**

Look at what wasm-bindgen generates for your original signature (`wasm-bindgen --target web` then check the `.js` glue):

```js
export function parse_points(input) {
    const ptr0 = passStringToWasm0(input, wasm.__wbindgen_malloc, ...);
    const len0 = WASM_VECTOR_LEN;
    const ret = wasm.parse_points(ptr0, len0);
    var v1 = getArrayJsValueFromWasm0(ret[0], ret[1]).slice();
    wasm.__wbindgen_free(ret[0], ret[1] * 4, 4);
    return v1;
}
```

Each element in `v1` is a `Point.__wrap(ptr)` — a JS object holding a pointer into linear memory. You MUST call `.free()` on every one, or the memory leaks for the life of the instance. For a parsed-data use case you almost certainly want plain JS objects:

```rust
use serde::Serialize;

#[derive(Serialize)]
pub struct Point { pub x: f64, pub y: f64 }

#[wasm_bindgen]
pub fn parse_points(input: &str) -> Result<JsValue, JsError> {
    let pts: Vec<Point> = input.split(',')
        .map(|s| s.trim().parse::<f64>().map(|n| Point { x: n, y: n*2.0 }))
        .collect::<Result<_, _>>()
        .map_err(|e| JsError::new(&e.to_string()))?;
    Ok(serde_wasm_bindgen::to_value(&pts)?)
}
```

Now JS gets a real array of `{x, y}` objects — no wrappers, no `.free()`, and structured-clone-able across workers.

**When to use each**

- `#[wasm_bindgen] struct` with exported methods: long-lived objects with behavior (a parser, a connection, a game state). Opaque handle, cheap calls.
- `serde-wasm-bindgen`: data-shaped returns. One alloc, one copy, done.

Rule of thumb: if JS is going to `JSON.stringify` it, use serde. If JS is going to call methods on it, use `#[wasm_bindgen]`.

There are two useful abstractions at different levels. I'd actually reach for the smaller one first — it composes better.

### `useDebouncedValue` — the primitive

```tsx
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

export function useDebouncedValue<T>(value: T, delayMs: number): T {
  const [debounced, setDebounced] = useState(value);

  useEffect(() => {
    const timer = setTimeout(() => setDebounced(value), delayMs);
    return () => clearTimeout(timer);
  }, [value, delayMs]);

  return debounced;
}
```

Usage with React Query, which is probably what you actually want in a real app:

```tsx
import { useQuery } from '@tanstack/react-query';

function Search() {
  const [q, setQ] = useState('');
  const debouncedQ = useDebouncedValue(q, 300);

  const query = useQuery({
    queryKey: ['search', debouncedQ],
    queryFn: ({ signal }) =>
      fetch(`/api/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(debouncedQ)}`, { signal })
        .then(r => r.json() as Promise<string[]>),
    enabled: debouncedQ.length > 0,
  });

  return (
    <>
      <input value={q} onChange={e => setQ(e.target.value)} />
      {query.status === 'success' && <Results items={query.data} />}
    </>
  );
}
```

This is the cleanest version because React Query already handles abort signals, deduplication, caching, and stale-while-revalidate. The hook is just "don't change my key on every keystroke."

### `useDebouncedFetch` — the higher-level option

If you don't have React Query and don't want to add it, here's a self-contained hook with a discriminated-union state:

```tsx
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from 'react';

type FetchState<T> =
  | { status: 'idle' }
  | { status: 'loading' }
  | { status: 'success'; data: T }
  | { status: 'error'; error: Error };

export function useDebouncedFetch<T>(
  url: string | null,
  delayMs: number,
  parse: (response: Response) => Promise<T> = r => r.json() as Promise<T>,
): FetchState<T> {
  const [state, setState] = useState<FetchState<T>>({ status: 'idle' });
  const parseRef = useRef(parse);

  useEffect(() => {
    parseRef.current = parse;
  });

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!url) {
      setState({ status: 'idle' });
      return;
    }

    const controller = new AbortController();
    const timer = setTimeout(async () => {
      setState({ status: 'loading' });
      try {
        const res = await fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal });
        if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
        const data = await parseRef.current(res);
        setState({ status: 'success', data });
      } catch (err) {
        if ((err as Error).name === 'AbortError') return;
        setState({ status: 'error', error: err as Error });
      }
    }, delayMs);

    return () => {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      controller.abort();
    };
  }, [url, delayMs]);

  return state;
}
```

Usage:

```tsx
function Search() {
  const [q, setQ] = useState('');
  const url = q ? `/api/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(q)}` : null;
  const result = useDebouncedFetch<string[]>(url, 300);

  return (
    <>
      <input value={q} onChange={e => setQ(e.target.value)} />
      {result.status === 'loading' && <span>Searching...</span>}
      {result.status === 'error' && <span>Error: {result.error.message}</span>}
      {result.status === 'success' && <Results items={result.data} />}
    </>
  );
}
```

### Which to pick

Go with `useDebouncedValue` + React Query if you have React Query (or are willing to add it). It's smaller, more composable, and you get caching + dedup for free. Reach for `useDebouncedFetch` when you specifically don't want a data-fetching library and want a one-stop hook. Don't write both — pick one shape and use it everywhere so people in your codebase don't have to context-switch.

A note on the parse-callback ref: I used the same `parseRef` trick from earlier so callers can pass an inline arrow without forcing the effect to re-run. Same pattern, applied generically.

This is the canonical stale closure bug. Your effect runs exactly once (empty deps), so `onKey` captures the `count` variable from the first render — where it's `0`. Every keypress calls `setCount(0 + 1)`, which is why you bounce between 0 and 1 forever.

The fix is the functional updater form of `setCount`:

```tsx
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

export function KeyCounter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    const onKey = () => {
      setCount(c => c + 1);
    };
    window.addEventListener('keydown', onKey);
    return () => window.removeEventListener('keydown', onKey);
  }, []);

  return <div>{count}</div>;
}
```

When you pass a function to `setCount`, React calls it with the *current* state at update time, sidestepping the closure entirely.

### Why not just add `count` to the dep array?

That technically works, but it tears down and re-attaches the keydown listener on every single increment. Functionally fine for one listener; wasteful and noisy in the React DevTools profiler. It also doesn't scale — once you have multiple stateful values referenced inside the handler, you're re-subscribing on every change.

### When you really do need the latest value of multiple things

If the handler legitimately needs current state/props that you don't want to re-subscribe over (e.g. a WebSocket message handler that reads three different state values), the standard pattern is a ref:

```tsx
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from 'react';

export function KeyCounter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const [enabled, setEnabled] = useState(true);

  const enabledRef = useRef(enabled);
  useEffect(() => { enabledRef.current = enabled; }, [enabled]);

  useEffect(() => {
    const onKey = () => {
      if (!enabledRef.current) return;
      setCount(c => c + 1);
    };
    window.addEventListener('keydown', onKey);
    return () => window.removeEventListener('keydown', onKey);
  }, []);

  return <div>{count}</div>;
}
```

The ref is mutable and always points at the latest value, so the listener (still attached once) reads fresh data every time.

For your specific bug, though, just use `setCount(c => c + 1)` and move on. The mental model to internalize: **closures inside `useEffect` see whatever was in scope when the effect ran**, not the latest render's values.

Memoization is caching the results of expensive function calls so that subsequent calls with the same arguments return the cached result instead of recomputing.

```python
# Without memoization: O(2^n) — exponential
def fib(n):
    if n < 2: return n
    return fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)  # fib(30) makes ~2.7 million calls

# With memoization: O(n) — linear
from functools import lru_cache

@lru_cache(maxsize=None)
def fib(n):
    if n < 2: return n
    return fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)  # fib(30) makes 31 calls
```

**When to use it:**
- Pure functions (same input always produces same output)
- Expensive computations called repeatedly with the same arguments
- Recursive algorithms with overlapping subproblems (dynamic programming)
- API calls or database queries with stable results

**When NOT to use it:**
- Functions with side effects (logging, database writes)
- Functions whose results change over time (current timestamp)
- Arguments that aren't hashable (lists, dicts — use tuples/frozensets instead)
- When memory is constrained (unbounded caches can consume lots of RAM)

**In JavaScript:**
```javascript
function memoize(fn) {
    const cache = new Map();
    return function(...args) {
        const key = JSON.stringify(args);
        if (cache.has(key)) return cache.get(key);
        const result = fn.apply(this, args);
        cache.set(key, result);
        return result;
    };
}
```

React's `useMemo` and `useCallback` are specialized forms of memoization for preventing unnecessary re-renders.

The clearest metaclass use case is a declarative schema system where class attributes define fields, and the act of defining the class triggers registration and validation. Here is a working implementation:

```python
from __future__ import annotations
from typing import Any, get_type_hints


class Field:
    """Descriptor representing a validated schema field."""

    def __init__(self, *, required: bool = True, default: Any = None) -> None:
        self.required = required
        self.default = default
        self.name: str = ""  # set by metaclass

    def __set_name__(self, owner: type, name: str) -> None:
        self.name = name

    def __repr__(self) -> str:
        return f"Field(name={self.name!r}, required={self.required})"


# Global registry mapping model names to classes.
_model_registry: dict[str, type] = {}


class ModelMeta(type):
    """Metaclass that collects Field descriptors and registers models."""

    def __new__(
        mcs,
        name: str,
        bases: tuple[type, ...],
        namespace: dict[str, Any],
    ) -> ModelMeta:
        cls = super().__new__(mcs, name, bases, namespace)

        # Collect all Field instances from the class and its bases.
        fields: dict[str, Field] = {}
        for klass in reversed(cls.__mro__):
            for attr_name, attr_value in vars(klass).items():
                if isinstance(attr_value, Field):
                    fields[attr_name] = attr_value
        cls._fields = fields  # type: ignore[attr-defined]

        # Register non-base classes.
        if bases:  # skip the base Model class itself
            if name in _model_registry:
                raise TypeError(f"Duplicate model name: {name!r}")
            _model_registry[name] = cls

        return cls  # type: ignore[return-value]


class Model(metaclass=ModelMeta):
    """Base class for declarative models."""

    def __init__(self, **kwargs: Any) -> None:
        for field_name, field in self._fields.items():  # type: ignore[attr-defined]
            if field_name in kwargs:
                setattr(self, field_name, kwargs[field_name])
            elif field.required:
                raise ValueError(
                    f"Missing required field {field_name!r} for {type(self).__name__}"
                )
            else:
                setattr(self, field_name, field.default)

    def to_dict(self) -> dict[str, Any]:
        return {name: getattr(self, name) for name in self._fields}  # type: ignore[attr-defined]

    def __repr__(self) -> str:
        pairs = ", ".join(f"{k}={v!r}" for k, v in self.to_dict().items())
        return f"{type(self).__name__}({pairs})"


# --- Usage ---

class User(Model):
    name = Field()
    email = Field()
    role = Field(required=False, default="viewer")


class Article(Model):
    title = Field()
    body = Field()
    published = Field(required=False, default=False)


# Metaclass auto-registered both models:
print(_model_registry)
# {'User': <class '__main__.User'>, 'Article': <class '__main__.Article'>}

u = User(name="Ada", email="ada@example.com")
print(u)            # User(name='Ada', email='ada@example.com', role='viewer')
print(u.to_dict())  # {'name': 'Ada', 'email': 'ada@example.com', 'role': 'viewer'}

# Missing required field raises immediately:
try:
    User(name="incomplete")
except ValueError as e:
    print(e)  # Missing required field 'email' for User
```

The metaclass does three things that `__init_subclass__` alone cannot:

1. **Walks the full MRO** to collect inherited fields, building a merged `_fields` dict that respects override order.
2. **Registers each subclass** into a global registry at definition time, catching duplicate names before any instance is created.
3. **Intercepts class creation** at the earliest possible point (`__new__`), which lets you reject invalid class definitions before they enter the runtime.

That said, if you only need simple registration or field collection, prefer `__init_subclass__` (Python 3.6+) or `dataclasses` (3.7+). Reach for a metaclass when you need to control how the class namespace is constructed or need to enforce invariants across an entire class hierarchy.

Not a compiler bug — your loop has undefined behavior and the optimizer is exploiting it. Signed integer overflow is UB in C++ (and C). Unsigned overflow is well-defined (wraps modulo 2^N); signed is not. That's a standards decision, and modern optimizers use it aggressively.

Here's what happens at -O2. GCC's value-range propagation sees `x * 2` with `int x`. It reasons: "if this doesn't overflow, then when `x > 0`, `x * 2 > 0`." Since overflow is UB and the optimizer is allowed to assume the program has no UB, that implication becomes an axiom. Substituting back into `while (x > 0)`, the condition is invariant-true for all iterations, so the loop never exits. GCC emits an infinite loop (on x86-64, something like `.L2: jmp .L2`). At -O0, no such inference: the `imull` instruction runs, wraps to a negative int, the loop exits, and you get 31.

This is the same mechanism behind the famous Linux kernel CVE where a null check was deleted because the pointer had been dereferenced on the line above — the dereference would have been UB if the pointer were null, so the compiler assumed it wasn't, and the subsequent null check became dead code.

To see it for yourself, run with UBSan:

```
clang++ -O2 -fsanitize=signed-integer-overflow -fno-sanitize-recover=all overflow.cpp
./a.out
```

You'll get something like:

```
overflow.cpp:6:15: runtime error: signed integer overflow: 1073741824 * 2 cannot be represented in type 'int'
SUMMARY: UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer: undefined-behavior
```

The fix depends on what you actually want:

**If you want wrapping semantics**, use unsigned. `unsigned int x` with `x *= 2` wraps cleanly and well-definedly to 0, and `while (x > 0)` works:

```cpp
unsigned int x = 1;
int count = 0;
while (x > 0) { x *= 2; ++count; }  // count = 32, then x wraps to 0
```

**If you want to count doublings that fit in a positive int**, test before you multiply:

```cpp
int x = 1;
int count = 0;
while (x <= std::numeric_limits<int>::max() / 2) {
    x *= 2;
    ++count;
}
// count = 30; the next doubling would overflow
```

**If you want portable checked arithmetic**, use the builtins (`__builtin_mul_overflow` — GCC/Clang, same on MSVC via `SafeInt` or C++26's planned `<checked_arithmetic>`):

```cpp
int x = 1, next;
int count = 0;
while (!__builtin_mul_overflow(x, 2, &next) && next > 0) {
    x = next;
    ++count;
}
```

**Escape hatch**: `-fwrapv` tells GCC/Clang to define signed overflow as two's-complement wrap. Your original code then terminates at -O2. But this disables optimizations that depend on UB (loop induction variable narrowing, some bounds-check elimination) and it's non-portable in the sense that you're relying on a flag, not the language. Use it for porting legacy code, not for new code.

The mental model to internalize: UB isn't "unspecified result" — it's a promise you made to the compiler that this case can't happen. The optimizer uses that promise like a theorem. If you break the promise, the optimizer's deductions are still valid from its perspective, but the resulting program does whatever falls out.

You have a spectrum of approaches:

**Manual (good for learning):**
```python
@bp.post('/users')
def create_user():
    data = request.get_json()
    if not data:
        raise APIError('Request body must be JSON', 400)

    errors = []
    if 'email' not in data:
        errors.append('email is required')
    if 'name' not in data:
        errors.append('name is required')
    if data.get('age') and not isinstance(data['age'], int):
        errors.append('age must be an integer')

    if errors:
        raise APIError({'validation_errors': errors}, 422)
```

**With a library (recommended for real projects):**
```python
from pydantic import BaseModel, EmailStr

class CreateUserRequest(BaseModel):
    name: str
    email: EmailStr
    age: int | None = None

@bp.post('/users')
def create_user():
    try:
        user_data = CreateUserRequest(**request.get_json())
    except ValidationError as e:
        raise APIError(e.errors(), 422)
```

Use 400 for malformed requests (bad JSON, wrong content type) and 422 for well-formed requests that fail business validation (missing field, invalid email). This distinction helps API consumers write better error handling on their end.

Here's the decision framework I actually use in code review, in priority order.

### The two-question filter

**Question 1: Do I actually need interior mutability?** Most of the time the answer is no — you can restructure to use `&mut self` and the problem goes away. Reach for interior mutability only when:
- You're behind a shared smart pointer (`Rc`/`Arc`) and can't get `&mut`.
- You need to mutate through `&self` to implement a trait that requires it (e.g. a cache inside a `Display` impl — rare and usually a smell).
- You have a graph-shaped data structure where multiple owners legitimately need to mutate.

**Question 2: Does this cross thread boundaries?** If yes, the only options are atomics, `Mutex`, or `RwLock` (`Cell` and `RefCell` are `!Sync`). If no, prefer `Cell` or `RefCell`.

### Cell: the free choice for `Copy` types

`Cell<T>` has literally zero runtime overhead — it's a compiler mechanism for telling the aliasing checker "trust me." Use it when `T: Copy` and you can get/set wholesale. Perfect for counters, flags, and `Option<Small>`.

```rust
use std::cell::Cell;

struct HitCounter {
    calls: Cell<u64>,
}

impl HitCounter {
    fn record(&self) {
        self.calls.set(self.calls.get() + 1);
    }
    fn total(&self) -> u64 { self.calls.get() }
}
```

Notice `record` takes `&self`, not `&mut self`. That's the whole point.

### RefCell: single-threaded caching and observer patterns

`RefCell<T>` gives you `&T` and `&mut T` through runtime-checked borrows. You pay a small cost (a borrow counter, a branch on each borrow) and a big risk: violating the borrow rules panics at runtime instead of failing at compile time.

The canonical right use: memoization inside an otherwise-immutable-looking API.

```rust
use std::cell::RefCell;
use std::collections::HashMap;

pub struct Resolver {
    cache: RefCell<HashMap<String, u64>>,
}

impl Resolver {
    pub fn lookup(&self, key: &str) -> u64 {
        if let Some(&v) = self.cache.borrow().get(key) {
            return v;
        }
        let v = expensive_compute(key);
        self.cache.borrow_mut().insert(key.to_owned(), v);
        v
    }
}
# fn expensive_compute(_: &str) -> u64 { 0 }
```

Anti-pattern: using `RefCell` because you didn't want to thread `&mut` through your call tree. That's laziness and it will bite you with a `BorrowMutError` panic in production.

### Mutex: the default cross-thread choice

For anything shared across threads, start with `Arc<Mutex<T>>`. It serializes all access (reads and writes), which sounds bad but has two virtues: predictable performance and no reader/writer starvation surprises.

```rust
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};
use std::thread;

let state = Arc::new(Mutex::new(Vec::<u32>::new()));
let handles: Vec<_> = (0..8)
    .map(|i| {
        let state = Arc::clone(&state);
        thread::spawn(move || {
            let mut guard = state.lock().unwrap();
            guard.push(i);
        })
    })
    .collect();
for h in handles { h.join().unwrap(); }
```

Handle poisoning deliberately — `lock()` returns `Result` because a panic inside a critical section may have left invariants broken. For most code, `.unwrap()` or `.expect("state lock poisoned")` is fine and the right thing to do.

### RwLock: reads must dominate *and* critical sections must be long

`RwLock` allows many concurrent readers or one writer. Intuition says "read-heavy workload → RwLock," but the per-operation overhead is meaningfully higher than `Mutex`. Benchmarks consistently show `Mutex` wins for short critical sections even with 99% reads, because the reader-count bookkeeping costs more than the mutex itself.

Use `RwLock` when:
- The critical section does real work (parsing a config, walking a structure), not just a hashmap lookup.
- Reads genuinely dominate (>100:1 read:write).
- You've *measured* that `Mutex` is the bottleneck.

```rust
use std::sync::{Arc, RwLock};

struct Config { /* many fields */ }
# impl Config { fn load() -> Self { Config {} } fn feature_x_enabled(&self) -> bool { true } }

let config = Arc::new(RwLock::new(Config::load()));

// Hot path: many threads read
let read = config.read().unwrap();
if read.feature_x_enabled() { /* ... */ }

// Rare: hot-reload
drop(read);
*config.write().unwrap() = Config::load();
```

Also consider `arc-swap` or `ArcSwap` for read-mostly config: it's lock-free for readers. For hot-reloadable config it's usually better than `RwLock`.

### The cheat sheet

| Situation | Use |
|---|---|
| Single-threaded, `T: Copy` | `Cell<T>` |
| Single-threaded, need `&mut T` | `RefCell<T>` |
| Cross-thread, default | `Arc<Mutex<T>>` |
| Cross-thread, measured read-heavy long critical sections | `Arc<RwLock<T>>` |
| Cross-thread, atomic primitive | `AtomicU64` etc. |
| Cross-thread, read-mostly config | `arc-swap` |

And the meta-rule: every time you reach for any of these, ask once more whether you can restructure to avoid it. Most "I need interior mutability" situations in Rust are really "I haven't figured out where the ownership should live."

Here are the patterns you'll actually use in Python, shown as idiomatic Python (not Java-style class hierarchies):

**1. Strategy — just pass a function:**
```python
def process_data(data, transform_fn):
    return [transform_fn(item) for item in data]

# Different strategies
process_data(items, str.upper)
process_data(items, lambda x: x.strip().lower())
process_data(items, my_custom_transform)
```

**2. Factory — function that returns the right object:**
```python
def create_parser(file_path: str):
    ext = Path(file_path).suffix
    parsers = {
        '.json': JSONParser,
        '.csv': CSVParser,
        '.xml': XMLParser,
    }
    parser_class = parsers.get(ext)
    if not parser_class:
        raise ValueError(f'No parser for {ext}')
    return parser_class(file_path)
```

**3. Decorator — Python has this built in:**
```python
import functools
import time

def cache_result(ttl_seconds=60):
    def decorator(func):
        cache = {}
        @functools.wraps(func)
        def wrapper(*args):
            now = time.time()
            if args in cache and now - cache[args][1] < ttl_seconds:
                return cache[args][0]
            result = func(*args)
            cache[args] = (result, now)
            return result
        return wrapper
    return decorator

@cache_result(ttl_seconds=300)
def fetch_user(user_id):
    return db.query(User).get(user_id)
```

**4. Observer — callbacks or events:**
```python
class EventEmitter:
    def __init__(self):
        self._handlers = defaultdict(list)

    def on(self, event, handler):
        self._handlers[event].append(handler)

    def emit(self, event, *args):
        for handler in self._handlers[event]:
            handler(*args)

# Usage
bus = EventEmitter()
bus.on('user_created', send_welcome_email)
bus.on('user_created', init_default_settings)
bus.emit('user_created', new_user)
```

Notice how none of these look like textbook Java patterns. Pythonic design patterns leverage first-class functions, decorators, and duck typing instead of abstract classes and interfaces.

`std::move` does not move anything. It is a cast. Understanding that is the key to everything else.

**The problem**

Consider a class that owns a heap buffer:

```cpp
class Buffer {
    char* data_;
    size_t size_;
public:
    Buffer(size_t n) : data_(new char[n]), size_(n) {}
    ~Buffer() { delete[] data_; }

    // Copy constructor — expensive, allocates and copies
    Buffer(const Buffer& other)
        : data_(new char[other.size_]), size_(other.size_) {
        std::memcpy(data_, other.data_, size_);
    }
};
```

If you return a `Buffer` from a function or push it into a vector, the copy constructor fires. For a 100MB buffer, that means allocating 100MB and copying it byte by byte — even if the original is about to be destroyed and its memory could just be reused.

**The solution: steal the guts**

A move constructor transfers ownership of the resource instead of copying it:

```cpp
// Move constructor — cheap, just pointer swaps
Buffer(Buffer&& other) noexcept
    : data_(other.data_), size_(other.size_) {
    other.data_ = nullptr;  // leave source in a valid but empty state
    other.size_ = 0;
}

// Move assignment operator
Buffer& operator=(Buffer&& other) noexcept {
    if (this != &other) {
        delete[] data_;          // free our current resource
        data_ = other.data_;     // steal theirs
        size_ = other.size_;
        other.data_ = nullptr;   // leave source empty
        other.size_ = 0;
    }
    return *this;
}
```

The `&&` is an **rvalue reference** — it binds to temporaries and to objects explicitly cast via `std::move`. The move constructor takes the pointer from the source, sets the source's pointer to null, and the destructor of the source safely calls `delete[] nullptr` (which is a no-op). Zero allocation, zero copying.

**What `std::move` actually does**

It is a `static_cast` to an rvalue reference. Nothing more:

```cpp
// Simplified — the real implementation handles forwarding references
template <typename T>
typename std::remove_reference<T>::type&& move(T&& arg) noexcept {
    return static_cast<typename std::remove_reference<T>::type&&>(arg);
}
```

When you write `Buffer b2 = std::move(b1);`, you are saying: "I am done with `b1`. Treat it as a temporary so the compiler selects the move constructor instead of the copy constructor." After the move, `b1` is in a valid but unspecified state — in our case, it holds a null pointer and size 0.

**When moves happen automatically:**
- Returning a local variable: `return buf;` — the compiler uses move (or NRVO eliminates the copy entirely)
- Passing a temporary: `process(Buffer(1024))` — the temporary is an rvalue
- `push_back` on a temporary: `vec.push_back(Buffer(1024))`

**When you need explicit `std::move`:**
- Transferring a named variable you no longer need: `vec.push_back(std::move(localBuf))`
- Passing ownership in a constructor: `MyClass(Buffer buf) : buf_(std::move(buf)) {}`

**The `noexcept` matters.** Mark move constructors `noexcept`. `std::vector` will fall back to copying during reallocation if the move constructor might throw, because it cannot safely restore the original state if a move throws halfway through.

The repository pattern abstracts data access behind a collection-like interface, separating business logic from database operations.

```python
from abc import ABC, abstractmethod

class UserRepository(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    async def get_by_id(self, user_id: int) -> User | None: ...

    @abstractmethod
    async def get_by_email(self, email: str) -> User | None: ...

    @abstractmethod
    async def save(self, user: User) -> User: ...

    @abstractmethod
    async def delete(self, user_id: int) -> bool: ...

class SQLUserRepository(UserRepository):
    def __init__(self, session: AsyncSession):
        self.session = session

    async def get_by_id(self, user_id: int) -> User | None:
        result = await self.session.execute(
            select(User).where(User.id == user_id)
        )
        return result.scalar_one_or_none()

    async def save(self, user: User) -> User:
        self.session.add(user)
        await self.session.flush()
        return user

# Business logic doesn't know about SQL
class UserService:
    def __init__(self, repo: UserRepository):
        self.repo = repo

    async def change_email(self, user_id: int, new_email: str):
        user = await self.repo.get_by_id(user_id)
        if not user:
            raise NotFoundError(f"User {user_id} not found")
        user.email = new_email
        await self.repo.save(user)
```

Benefits: business logic is testable without a database (inject a mock repository), you can switch databases without changing business logic, and data access patterns are centralized.

A trie stores strings in a tree where each node represents a character. Shared prefixes share nodes, making prefix-based operations fast.

```python
class TrieNode:
    def __init__(self):
        self.children: dict[str, 'TrieNode'] = {}
        self.is_end = False

class Trie:
    def __init__(self):
        self.root = TrieNode()

    def insert(self, word: str) -> None:
        node = self.root
        for char in word:
            if char not in node.children:
                node.children[char] = TrieNode()
            node = node.children[char]
        node.is_end = True

    def search(self, word: str) -> bool:
        node = self._find(word)
        return node is not None and node.is_end

    def starts_with(self, prefix: str) -> bool:
        return self._find(prefix) is not None

    def autocomplete(self, prefix: str, limit: int = 10) -> list[str]:
        node = self._find(prefix)
        if not node:
            return []
        results = []
        self._dfs(node, prefix, results, limit)
        return results

    def _find(self, prefix: str) -> TrieNode | None:
        node = self.root
        for char in prefix:
            if char not in node.children:
                return None
            node = node.children[char]
        return node

    def _dfs(self, node, current, results, limit):
        if len(results) >= limit:
            return
        if node.is_end:
            results.append(current)
        for char, child in sorted(node.children.items()):
            self._dfs(child, current + char, results, limit)

# Usage
t = Trie()
for word in ['apple', 'app', 'application', 'apply', 'banana']:
    t.insert(word)

t.search('app')       # True
t.starts_with('app')  # True
t.autocomplete('app') # ['app', 'apple', 'application', 'apply']
```

Use cases: autocomplete, spell checking, IP routing tables, phone dictionaries.

Sharding is straightforward. Wrap an array of `LRUCache` instances behind a router that hashes the key to pick a shard:

```go
package cache

import (
    "hash/fnv"
)

type ShardedLRU struct {
    shards []*LRUCache
    mask   uint32
}

// NewShardedLRU creates a sharded cache. shardCount must be a power of 2.
// totalCapacity is distributed evenly across shards.
func NewShardedLRU(shardCount, totalCapacity int) *ShardedLRU {
    if shardCount <= 0 || shardCount&(shardCount-1) != 0 {
        panic("shardCount must be a power of 2")
    }
    perShard := totalCapacity / shardCount
    if perShard < 1 {
        perShard = 1
    }

    shards := make([]*LRUCache, shardCount)
    for i := range shards {
        shards[i] = NewLRUCache(perShard)
    }

    return &ShardedLRU{
        shards: shards,
        mask:   uint32(shardCount - 1),
    }
}

func (s *ShardedLRU) shard(key string) *LRUCache {
    h := fnv.New32a()
    h.Write([]byte(key))
    return s.shards[h.Sum32()&s.mask]
}

func (s *ShardedLRU) Get(key string) ([]byte, bool) {
    return s.shard(key).Get(key)
}

func (s *ShardedLRU) Set(key string, val []byte) {
    s.shard(key).Set(key, val)
}

func (s *ShardedLRU) Len() int {
    total := 0
    for _, sh := range s.shards {
        total += sh.Len()
    }
    return total
}
```

Usage:

```go
// 256 shards, 1M total entries
c := NewShardedLRU(256, 1_000_000)
c.Set("user:123", data)
val, ok := c.Get("user:123")
```

With 256 shards, the probability of two concurrent operations hitting the same shard (and thus contending on the same mutex) drops to roughly 1/256. For most workloads this effectively eliminates contention.

The trade-off is that eviction is per-shard, not global. Each shard independently evicts its least-recently-used entry when full. This means the cache isn't a perfect global LRU — a shard that receives more traffic will evict more aggressively than a quiet shard. In practice, FNV distributes keys uniformly enough that this doesn't matter unless your key distribution is heavily skewed.

One more optimization to consider: if profiling shows that `fnv.New32a()` allocation is measurable at extreme QPS, you can use `maphash.String()` from Go 1.22+ which avoids allocation entirely. But measure before optimizing — FNV is already fast.

Union-find is exactly right for this. Here's why, then the code.

The operation profile of your dedup service:
- Stream of 'id A and id B are the same entity' assertions (merges).
- Occasional 'are A and B currently the same entity?' queries.
- At the end (or periodically), 'give me the cluster for each id.'

Union-find handles all three in **amortized O(alpha(n)) ~ O(1)** per operation, where alpha is the inverse Ackermann function (<=4 for any n you'll meet). The naive `Map<id, clusterId>` approach is O(min(|A|, |B|)) per merge because you must relabel one cluster's members. On adversarial input — many merges of growing clusters — it degrades to O(n^2) total.

The trick: **don't store cluster IDs. Store parent pointers and find the root on demand.** Two cheap optimizations make this fast:

1. **Path compression**: when you walk to a root, repoint every node along the way directly to the root.
2. **Union by size/rank**: when merging two trees, attach the smaller under the larger. Keeps trees shallow.

With both, the amortized bound is alpha(n). With neither, you get pathological O(n) chains.

```typescript
export class DSU {
  private parent: number[];
  private size: number[];
  private numComponents: number;

  constructor(n: number) {
    this.parent = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => i);
    this.size = new Array(n).fill(1);
    this.numComponents = n;
  }

  find(x: number): number {
    // Iterative path compression — avoids stack blowup on long chains
    let root = x;
    while (this.parent[root] !== root) root = this.parent[root];
    while (this.parent[x] !== root) {
      const next = this.parent[x];
      this.parent[x] = root;
      x = next;
    }
    return root;
  }

  union(a: number, b: number): boolean {
    const ra = this.find(a);
    const rb = this.find(b);
    if (ra === rb) return false;
    // Union by size: smaller tree hangs off larger tree's root
    const [small, large] = this.size[ra] < this.size[rb] ? [ra, rb] : [rb, ra];
    this.parent[small] = large;
    this.size[large] += this.size[small];
    this.numComponents--;
    return true;
  }

  connected(a: number, b: number): boolean {
    return this.find(a) === this.find(b);
  }

  componentSize(x: number): number {
    return this.size[this.find(x)];
  }

  components(): number {
    return this.numComponents;
  }

  addNode(): number {
    const i = this.parent.length;
    this.parent.push(i);
    this.size.push(1);
    this.numComponents++;
    return i;
  }
}
```

For your dedup service, you almost certainly have string IDs (UUIDs, emails, whatever), so wrap it:

```typescript
export class StringDSU {
  private idToIdx = new Map<string, number>();
  private idxToId: string[] = [];
  private dsu = new DSU(0);

  private idx(id: string): number {
    let i = this.idToIdx.get(id);
    if (i === undefined) {
      i = this.dsu.addNode();
      this.idToIdx.set(id, i);
      this.idxToId.push(id);
    }
    return i;
  }

  merge(a: string, b: string): boolean {
    return this.dsu.union(this.idx(a), this.idx(b));
  }

  sameCluster(a: string, b: string): boolean {
    return this.dsu.connected(this.idx(a), this.idx(b));
  }

  clusters(): Map<string, string[]> {
    const out = new Map<string, string[]>();
    for (let i = 0; i < this.idxToId.length; i++) {
      const root = this.idxToId[this.dsu.find(i)];
      const bucket = out.get(root) ?? [];
      bucket.push(this.idxToId[i]);
      out.set(root, bucket);
    }
    return out;
  }
}
```

### Production caveats

- **Undo**: standard DSU can't undo a merge cheaply. If you need rollback (e.g., 'this assertion was bad, retract it'), you need rollback-DSU which skips path compression and uses a stack of unions — O(log n) per op instead of alpha(n), but undo is O(1).
- **Persistence**: serialize as `{ parent: number[], size: number[], stringMap: ... }`. After deserializing, the structure works correctly even though the trees aren't fully compressed.
- **Sharding**: union-find doesn't shard well — entities can cross shard boundaries. If your stream is too big for one box, look at distributed connected-components (Pregel-style label propagation).

### Complexity

- `find`, `union`, `connected`: O(alpha(n)) amortized — effectively O(1).
- `clusters()`: O(n * alpha(n)).
- Space: O(n) for parent + size arrays, plus O(n) for the string <-> index map.

For a 100M-record dedup with billions of assertions, this will be CPU-bound on the Map lookups, not the DSU itself.

Three ingredients compose here. Mapped type, key remapping with `as`, and the `Capitalize` intrinsic wrapped in a template literal:

```ts
type Handlers<E> = {
  [K in keyof E as `on${Capitalize<K & string>}`]: (event: E[K]) => void;
};

type Events = {
  click: MouseEvent;
  keydown: KeyboardEvent;
  focus: FocusEvent;
};

type H = Handlers<Events>;
// {
//   onClick: (event: MouseEvent) => void;
//   onKeydown: (event: KeyboardEvent) => void;
//   onFocus: (event: FocusEvent) => void;
// }
```

The `& string` on `K` is the non-obvious bit. `keyof E` is `string | number | symbol`, and template literal types demand a string placeholder. Drop the intersection and you get:

```
Type 'K' does not satisfy the constraint 'string | number | bigint | boolean | null | undefined'.
  Type 'string | number | symbol' is not assignable to type '...'.
    Type 'number' is ...
```

`K & string` narrows to just the string keys (symbol keys silently drop, which is usually what you want for a public-ish API).

A few refinements worth knowing:

**Optional handlers.** Just add `?:`:

```ts
type Handlers<E> = {
  [K in keyof E as `on${Capitalize<K & string>}`]?: (event: E[K]) => void;
};
```

**Two-way: derive the event name back from a handler name.** Use `Uncapitalize` with a template literal pattern as an inferred placeholder:

```ts
type EventName<H extends string> =
  H extends `on${infer Rest}` ? Uncapitalize<Rest> : never;

type E1 = EventName<'onClick'>; // 'click'
```

**The word-boundary limit.** `Capitalize<'pointerdown'>` gives `'Pointerdown'`, not `'PointerDown'`. Template literal types have no concept of word boundaries in lowercase identifiers. If your events are `pointerdown`, `mouseenter`, etc., and you want `onPointerDown`, you have two realistic options:

1. **Keep the keys camelCase at the source**: rename `Events` to `{ pointerDown: ...; mouseEnter: ... }` and the `Capitalize` pass gives you the right output. This is usually the right move.
2. **Maintain an explicit alias map** when you can't control the source:
   ```ts
   type EventAliases = {
     pointerdown: 'PointerDown';
     mouseenter: 'MouseEnter';
   };
   type Handlers<E> = {
     [K in keyof E as `on${K extends keyof EventAliases ? EventAliases[K] : Capitalize<K & string>}`]: (e: E[K]) => void;
   };
   ```

Don't try to write a generic camelCase-splitter in the type system. You can do it with enough recursive template literal gymnastics, but you'll spike compile times and the error messages degrade to `Type instantiation is excessively deep`. The explicit map is better engineering.

One last thing. If you use this pattern to generate a *builder* (object passed to an emitter), `keyof Handlers<E>` round-trips cleanly: `keyof Handlers<Events>` is `'onClick' | 'onKeydown' | 'onFocus'`. That composes well with autocomplete and `satisfies` checks at call sites.

**Level 1: Component state (useState)**
```jsx
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
```
Good for: UI state local to one component (form inputs, toggles, counters)

**Level 2: Shared state (lift state up)**
```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <>
      <Header user={user} />
      <Profile user={user} onUpdate={setUser} />
    </>
  );
}
```
Good for: State shared between siblings or parent-child

**Level 3: Context (useContext)**
```jsx
const ThemeContext = createContext('light');
// Provider at top, consumers anywhere below
```
Good for: Low-frequency updates used by many components (theme, auth, locale)

**Level 4: External store (Zustand, Redux, Jotai)**
```jsx
const useStore = create((set) => ({
  items: [],
  addItem: (item) => set((s) => ({ items: [...s.items, item] })),
}));
```
Good for: Complex state, frequent updates, state shared across the whole app

**Decision flow:**
1. Can it be local? → useState
2. Shared between a few components? → Lift state up
3. App-wide, changes rarely? → Context
4. App-wide, changes often, complex logic? → External store (Zustand is a good default)

Yes, exactly the same trap, and arguably worse because grid track sizing is more opaque. Grid items also default to `min-width: auto` / `min-height: auto`, so a `1fr` column will refuse to shrink below the largest intrinsic content size of any item in that column.

The canonical fix is `minmax(0, 1fr)` instead of `1fr` on the track:

```css
.layout {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 240px minmax(0, 1fr);
}
```

`minmax(0, 1fr)` says "this track can be as small as 0 and as large as 1 fraction of available space," overriding the implicit min-content floor. This is the form you want roughly 80% of the time when you write `1fr` and have any chance of long content.

Alternatively, set `min-width: 0` on the grid item itself:

```css
.main {
    min-width: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
}
```

Both work; `minmax(0, 1fr)` is cleaner because it's a single change at the layout level. Once you've used grid for a while, you'll find yourself reflexively writing `minmax(0, 1fr)` as the default. The bare `1fr` is really only correct when you specifically want "share space, but never below content's natural width" — which is unusual in responsive layouts.

There's also a related grid-specific gotcha: `auto` track size. `grid-template-columns: 240px auto` will size the second track to its content's max-content size, which has the same blowout problem. Use `1fr` (with the minmax fix) when you want the "fill remaining space" behavior.

The overall takeaway: any sizing context that allows children to influence container size — flex, grid, and to a lesser extent inline-block layouts — inherits this min-content floor by default. The opt-out (`min-width: 0` / `min-height: 0` / `minmax(0, ...)`) is one of the most-needed CSS patterns nobody mentions in tutorials.

Honest answer: probably write it yourself if your modal hosts known content, keep the library if it hosts user-arbitrary content. Here's the breakdown.

**What you're paying 8KB for:**

- Correct tabbable-element selector (it's gnarlier than `[tabindex]` — has to handle disabled, hidden, contenteditable, audio/video[controls], radio groups where only the checked one is tabbable).
- Mutation observation (refocus when a new input appears mid-modal).
- Shadow DOM traversal.
- iframe edge cases.
- Sensible 'where to focus first' heuristics.

If your modals contain forms you wrote, dialogs you control, and standard widgets, you don't need most of that. If they host third-party embeds, video players with custom controls, or rich-text editors — keep the lib.

**Hand-rolled minimal trap** (~30 lines):

```tsx
function useFocusTrap(active: boolean, ref: React.RefObject<HTMLElement>) {
  useEffect(() => {
    if (!active || !ref.current) return;
    const root = ref.current;

    const SELECTOR =
      'a[href], button:not([disabled]), input:not([disabled]):not([type="hidden"]),' +
      ' select:not([disabled]), textarea:not([disabled]), [tabindex]:not([tabindex="-1"])';

    const getTabbable = () =>
      Array.from(root.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(SELECTOR)).filter(
        (el) => !el.hasAttribute('disabled') && el.offsetParent !== null,
      );

    // Initial focus.
    const tabbable = getTabbable();
    (tabbable[0] ?? root).focus();

    const onKey = (e: KeyboardEvent) => {
      if (e.key !== 'Tab') return;
      const items = getTabbable();
      if (items.length === 0) {
        e.preventDefault();
        return;
      }
      const first = items[0];
      const last = items[items.length - 1];
      const active = document.activeElement;
      if (e.shiftKey && active === first) {
        e.preventDefault();
        last.focus();
      } else if (!e.shiftKey && active === last) {
        e.preventDefault();
        first.focus();
      }
    };

    root.addEventListener('keydown', onKey);
    return () => root.removeEventListener('keydown', onKey);
  }, [active, ref]);
}
```

**Things this misses** (probably acceptable):

- Radio groups: tabs to all radios instead of just the checked one. Annoying but not broken.
- New focusable elements added after mount: won't be picked up until next Tab.
- Shadow DOM: `querySelectorAll` doesn't pierce. Fine if you don't use it.

**Before you commit to either path**, run `source-map-explorer` (or `webpack-bundle-analyzer` / `vite-bundle-visualizer`) on your prod bundle. I'd bet there's a 50KB icon set or moment.js you can shave first; 8KB of focus-trap is rounding error compared to those. If the hand-rolled version covers your content profile and you've already optimized the obvious wins, sure, save the bytes. But don't make this the first thing you cut.

Ship the command pattern first. Here's the memory math that drove me there, then a skeleton you can extend.

**Memory cost, worked through**

Assume 1,000 shapes on canvas, avg 500 B each, so ~500 KB document. History depth 500.

| Approach | Per-entry cost | 500-entry total | Worst-case spike |
|---|---|---|---|
| Immer snapshots, single-shape ops | ~1 KB (structural sharing) | ~500 KB | Multi-select or canvas-wide op: 25 KB–500 KB per entry, total can blow past 50 MB |
| Command pattern | ~100 B (op + inverse) | ~50 KB | Delete-all: inverse stores every shape, one-time ~500 KB |
| CRDT (Yjs) | Full op retained; overhead on tombstones | 5–10× document size for heavily edited docs | Unbounded until GC |

Two orders of magnitude between commands and snapshots. On mobile or low-memory devices, that gap matters. Even when it doesn't, commands give you things snapshots can't:

- **Coalescing.** A drag is 200 mousemove events; users expect one undo, not 200.
- **Serializable history** — log ops to disk, replay for bug reports, resume after refresh.
- **Cheap multiplayer migration** — your commands become ops over the wire when you add sync.

**Skeleton**

```ts
interface Shape {
  id: string;
  x: number;
  y: number;
  width: number;
  height: number;
  fill: string;
}

interface Doc {
  shapes: Record<string, Shape>;
  order: string[];
}

// A Command knows how to apply itself and produce its inverse given the pre-state.
interface Command {
  readonly type: string;
  apply(doc: Doc): Doc;
  invert(preState: Doc): Command;
}

class MoveShapes implements Command {
  readonly type = 'move';
  constructor(private ids: string[], private dx: number, private dy: number) {}
  apply(doc: Doc): Doc {
    const shapes = { ...doc.shapes };
    for (const id of this.ids) {
      const s = shapes[id];
      if (s) shapes[id] = { ...s, x: s.x + this.dx, y: s.y + this.dy };
    }
    return { ...doc, shapes };
  }
  invert(): Command {
    return new MoveShapes(this.ids, -this.dx, -this.dy);
  }
  // Coalesce with a continuing drag of the same selection.
  tryMerge(other: Command): Command | null {
    if (other instanceof MoveShapes && sameSet(this.ids, other.ids)) {
      return new MoveShapes(this.ids, this.dx + other.dx, this.dy + other.dy);
    }
    return null;
  }
}

class DeleteShapes implements Command {
  readonly type = 'delete';
  constructor(private ids: string[]) {}
  apply(doc: Doc): Doc {
    const shapes = { ...doc.shapes };
    for (const id of this.ids) delete shapes[id];
    return { ...doc, shapes, order: doc.order.filter((id) => !this.ids.includes(id)) };
  }
  invert(preState: Doc): Command {
    // Inverse must capture the deleted shapes' data.
    const captured = this.ids
      .map((id) => ({ id, shape: preState.shapes[id], index: preState.order.indexOf(id) }))
      .filter((x) => x.shape);
    return new RestoreShapes(captured);
  }
}

class RestoreShapes implements Command {
  readonly type = 'restore';
  constructor(private entries: { id: string; shape: Shape; index: number }[]) {}
  apply(doc: Doc): Doc {
    const shapes = { ...doc.shapes };
    const order = [...doc.order];
    for (const { id, shape, index } of this.entries) {
      shapes[id] = shape;
      order.splice(Math.min(index, order.length), 0, id);
    }
    return { ...doc, shapes, order };
  }
  invert(): Command {
    return new DeleteShapes(this.entries.map((e) => e.id));
  }
}

function sameSet(a: string[], b: string[]): boolean {
  if (a.length !== b.length) return false;
  const s = new Set(a);
  return b.every((x) => s.has(x));
}
```

**The history controller with coalescing**

```ts
interface HistoryEntry {
  command: Command;
  inverse: Command;
}

class History {
  private past: HistoryEntry[] = [];
  private future: HistoryEntry[] = [];
  private readonly maxDepth = 500;

  execute(doc: Doc, command: Command, coalesce = false): Doc {
    const inverse = command.invert(doc);
    const next = command.apply(doc);

    if (coalesce && this.past.length > 0) {
      const top = this.past[this.past.length - 1];
      const merged = (top.command as any).tryMerge?.(command);
      if (merged) {
        // Keep the older inverse (undoes back to pre-drag start), replace forward op.
        this.past[this.past.length - 1] = { command: merged, inverse: top.inverse };
        this.future = [];
        return next;
      }
    }

    this.past.push({ command, inverse });
    if (this.past.length > this.maxDepth) this.past.shift();
    this.future = [];
    return next;
  }

  undo(doc: Doc): Doc {
    const entry = this.past.pop();
    if (!entry) return doc;
    this.future.push(entry);
    return entry.inverse.apply(doc);
  }

  redo(doc: Doc): Doc {
    const entry = this.future.pop();
    if (!entry) return doc;
    this.past.push(entry);
    return entry.command.apply(doc);
  }
}
```

A drag calls `execute(doc, new MoveShapes(ids, dx, dy), /*coalesce=*/ true)` on every mousemove. They merge into a single history entry. On mouseup, call once more with `coalesce = false` to seal the entry so the next action doesn't fold into it.

**When snapshots would win**

Small app, state under 10 KB, no drag-like operations, no plans for sync — snapshots via `useState` or Zustand's middleware are five lines of code and fine. For a 'Figma-lite' you described, you outgrow that by week two.

**When to reach for the CRDT**

When you're ready to ship multiplayer. Your command log is already 90% of what you need: each command becomes a Yjs transaction, and you rely on Yjs's own undo manager scoped to the local user. Don't put a CRDT under single-player undo — you'll pay memory and complexity costs for a feature you aren't shipping yet.

`useMemo` caches a **value**. `useCallback` caches a **function**.

```jsx
// useMemo — expensive computation
const sorted = useMemo(() => {
  return items.sort((a, b) => a.price - b.price);
}, [items]);

// useCallback — stable function reference
const handleClick = useCallback((id) => {
  setSelected(id);
}, []); // Same function object across renders
```

**`useCallback(fn, deps)` is exactly `useMemo(() => fn, deps)`.**

**When to use:**
- `useMemo`: Expensive calculations that shouldn't re-run on every render
- `useCallback`: Functions passed as props to `memo()`-wrapped children, or as dependencies of other hooks

**When NOT to use:**
```jsx
// Don't memoize cheap operations
const fullName = useMemo(() => `${first} ${last}`, [first, last]); // Overkill
const fullName = `${first} ${last}`; // Just compute it

// Don't memoize unless a child actually uses React.memo
const handleClick = useCallback(() => {}, []); // Useless if Child isn't memoized
```

Rule: Don't optimize until you measure. Most React performance issues are structural (too many components re-rendering), not computational.

This is harder than it looks. **Don't put a timing-measurement test in CI** — GC pauses, scheduler jitter, and CPU frequency scaling make microbenchmarks unreliable enough that the test will either flake constantly or set the threshold so loose that it doesn't catch real regressions. The right answer is two layers: a fast structural check that catches the obvious regression, and an offline statistical test for confidence.

**Layer 1 — structural lint**, runs on every PR:

```yaml
# .semgrep/timing-safe.yml
rules:
  - id: insecure-mac-comparison
    message: |
      HMAC/signature values must be compared with subtle.ConstantTimeCompare,
      not == or bytes.Equal.
    languages: [go]
    severity: ERROR
    pattern-either:
      - pattern: bytes.Equal($X, $Y.Sum(...))
      - pattern: bytes.Equal($X.Sum(...), $Y)
      - pattern: string($X) == string($Y.Sum(...))
      - patterns:
          - pattern: $X == $Y
          - metavariable-pattern:
              metavariable: $X
              patterns:
                - pattern-either:
                    - pattern-regex: '(?i).*(signature|hmac|mac|tag).*'
```

Follow that with a custom analyzer using `go/analysis` if you want stricter typing:

```go
// tools/lint/timingsafe/timingsafe.go
func run(pass *analysis.Pass) (interface{}, error) {
    inspector := pass.ResultOf[inspect.Analyzer].(*inspector.Inspector)
    inspector.Preorder([]ast.Node{(*ast.BinaryExpr)(nil)}, func(n ast.Node) {
        be := n.(*ast.BinaryExpr)
        if be.Op != token.EQL && be.Op != token.NEQ { return }
        // Check if either side is a hash.Hash.Sum(...) result
        if isMacResult(pass, be.X) || isMacResult(pass, be.Y) {
            pass.Reportf(be.Pos(), "comparison of MAC value must use subtle.ConstantTimeCompare")
        }
    })
    return nil, nil
}
```

Wire it into your `golangci-lint` config and the regression gets blocked at code-review time.

**Layer 2 — DUDECT-style statistical test**, runs offline (locally or in a dedicated low-noise machine, not shared CI):

```go
// timing_test.go — run with: go test -run TestTimingDistribution -count=1 -timeout 30m
func TestTimingDistribution(t *testing.T) {
    if testing.Short() {
        t.Skip("timing test, run explicitly")
    }

    secret := []byte("test-secret-32-bytes-long-padding-pad")
    body := []byte(`{"id":"evt_123"}`)
    expected := computeSig(secret, body)

    // Population A: signature mismatches at byte 0
    bad0 := append([]byte{}, expected...)
    bad0[0] ^= 0xff

    // Population B: signature mismatches at byte 31 (last byte)
    bad31 := append([]byte{}, expected...)
    bad31[31] ^= 0xff

    const n = 200_000
    timesA := make([]float64, n)
    timesB := make([]float64, n)

    for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
        // Interleave to dampen long-term drift
        if i%2 == 0 {
            timesA[i] = measure(func() { verifySig(body, hex.EncodeToString(bad0), secret) })
            timesB[i] = measure(func() { verifySig(body, hex.EncodeToString(bad31), secret) })
        } else {
            timesB[i] = measure(func() { verifySig(body, hex.EncodeToString(bad31), secret) })
            timesA[i] = measure(func() { verifySig(body, hex.EncodeToString(bad0), secret) })
        }
    }

    // Trim outliers (top 10%) — those are GC/scheduler events, not signal
    timesA = trimUpper(timesA, 0.1)
    timesB = trimUpper(timesB, 0.1)

    // Welch's t-test on trimmed means
    t_stat := welchT(timesA, timesB)

    // |t| > 4.5 corresponds to p < 1e-5 — clear signal of timing dependence on byte position
    if math.Abs(t_stat) > 4.5 {
        t.Fatalf("timing leak detected: t-statistic = %.2f (means A=%.1fns B=%.1fns)",
                 t_stat, mean(timesA), mean(timesB))
    }
    t.Logf("timing test passed: t = %.2f (no significant difference)", t_stat)
}

func measure(f func()) float64 {
    runtime.GC()
    start := time.Now()
    f()
    return float64(time.Since(start).Nanoseconds())
}
```

This is genuinely robust if you run it with N≥100k samples on a quiet machine. The trimmed-mean + Welch's t-test pattern is what DUDECT uses; it tolerates the fat tails that come from GC pauses without losing sensitivity to a real timing dependence. With a constant-time compare you'll see |t| < 2 reliably; with a regression to `bytes.Equal` you'll see |t| > 20 within seconds.

**Don't run this in PR CI** — even on dedicated runners, the overhead of containerization plus shared hosts will produce false positives. Run it nightly on a dedicated bare-metal host or as a manual gate before releases that touch crypto code. Ship the lint check in CI; ship the statistical test in your release process.

A function that processes a template literal, receiving the string parts and interpolated values separately.

```javascript
function sql(strings, ...values) {
    // strings: ['SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ', ' AND active = ', '']
    // values: [42, true]
    return { query: strings.join('?'), params: values };
}

const result = sql`SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ${42} AND active = ${true}`;
// { query: 'SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ? AND active = ?', params: [42, true] }
```

Used for: SQL injection prevention, CSS-in-JS (styled-components), i18n, syntax highlighting.

The technique is called **seam extraction** — finding the narrowest point where you can break a dependency for testing without restructuring everything.

**Quick approach: `@MockBean` at the boundary**

If `OrderService` calls `InventoryClient` (which hits an external API), mock the external boundary:

```java
@SpringBootTest
class OrderServiceIT {

    @MockBean
    InventoryClient inventoryClient; // replaces the real bean

    @Autowired
    OrderService orderService;

    @Test
    void placeOrder_sufficientStock_succeeds() {
        when(inventoryClient.checkStock("SKU-100", 5))
            .thenReturn(new StockResult(true, 42));

        Order order = orderService.place("SKU-100", 5, "cust_456");

        assertThat(order.status()).isEqualTo(OrderStatus.CONFIRMED);
        verify(inventoryClient).checkStock("SKU-100", 5);
    }
}
```

**Cleaner long-term: constructor injection + plain Mockito**

If the class uses field injection (`@Autowired` on the field), refactor to constructor injection — this is a safe, mechanical refactoring:

```java
// Before: untestable without Spring context
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @Autowired
    private InventoryClient inventory;
    @Autowired
    private PaymentService payments;
}

// After: testable with plain Mockito, no Spring context needed
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final InventoryClient inventory;
    private final PaymentService payments;

    public OrderService(InventoryClient inventory, PaymentService payments) {
        this.inventory = inventory;
        this.payments = payments;
    }
}
```

Now you can write fast unit tests without Spring:

```java
class OrderServiceTest {
    private final InventoryClient inventory = mock(InventoryClient.class);
    private final PaymentService payments = mock(PaymentService.class);
    private final OrderService sut = new OrderService(inventory, payments);

    @Test
    void placeOrder_insufficientStock_throwsException() {
        when(inventory.checkStock(any(), anyInt()))
            .thenReturn(new StockResult(false, 0));

        assertThatThrownBy(() -> sut.place("SKU-100", 5, "cust_456"))
            .isInstanceOf(InsufficientStockException.class);
    }
}
```

**Rule of thumb:** mock at the boundary, not in the middle. Mock external APIs, databases, message queues. Do not mock your own service classes unless absolutely necessary — that leads to tests that pass even when the code is wrong.

The 'second iteration' detail is the key clue — that's not a bug in a single parse, it's *state leaking between parses*. Three concrete suspects in order of likelihood:

### Suspect 1 (most likely): static state in the parser

Grep the parser for `static`:

```bash
grep -n '^static\|^\s*static' parser.c
```

If you find static caches, arenas, or 'last message' pointers, that's almost certainly it. Pattern:

```c
static tlv_field_pool g_pool;       // <-- the trap

int tlv_parse(const uint8_t *buf, size_t len, tlv_message **out) {
    // Sub-objects allocated from g_pool, pointers stashed in *out.
    ...
}

void tlv_free(tlv_message *msg) {
    // Frees pool entries directly via free(), not back to the pool.
    // After this, g_pool's bookkeeping points at freed memory.
}
```

Next call to `tlv_parse` reuses the pool, sees a 'free slot' that's actually a freed-to-libc chunk, hands you a pointer to the same address, and the next `tlv_free` writes through that pointer. That's your WRITE of 8.

Fix shape: pool entries owned by the pool, `tlv_free` returns to pool, parser uninitialized between calls — or, simpler, kill the pool and just use plain malloc/free.

### Suspect 2: shared sub-object between messages

The parser interns string values (or tags, or fields) into a shared dictionary, and `tlv_free` frees them directly:

```c
struct tlv_message {
    char **field_names;   // <-- if these are shared, freeing them is wrong
    uint8_t **field_data;
    size_t n_fields;
};
```

Second message references the same interned string. First `tlv_free` releases it. Second `tlv_free` walks the second message and writes to (or frees again) the same address.

Diagnostic: in your harness, after `tlv_parse` succeeds, log the pointer values of any sub-fields. Run with the same input twice. If the pointers match across iterations, it's sharing.

### Suspect 3: tlv_message keeps a pointer into the input buffer

libFuzzer reuses the input buffer for each `LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput` call (different content, same address). If `tlv_message` stores `field_data = data + offset` (no copy), then on iter 2 those pointers are valid (same buffer) but reference different bytes. That's not the UAF directly, but `tlv_free` might also try to `free()` those pointers thinking they're heap — which is **freeing a non-heap pointer**, which ASan reports as UAF inside whatever the allocator returned next.

Diagnostic: check if `tlv_free` calls `free(field->data)` for field data. If yes, and the parser doesn't `memcpy` field bytes, that's the bug.

### How to read the ASan output to confirm

The report should include both stacks:

```
freed by thread T0 here:
    #0 free <-- somewhere in tlv_free, parser.c:218
    #1 ...
previously allocated by thread T0 here:
    #0 malloc <-- in tlv_parse, parser.c:91
```

The `freed by` line tells you which `tlv_free` call line freed the chunk. The `previously allocated` line tells you where in `tlv_parse` it was malloc'd. Compare across iterations — the 'freed by' should be from iteration 1, the current write is iteration 2. That confirms cross-iteration leakage.

Increase stack depth if needed: `ASAN_OPTIONS=malloc_context_size=30:abort_on_error=1`.

### Make the diagnosis explicit

Add this to the harness and rerun:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
static int iter = 0;

int LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput(const uint8_t *data, size_t size) {
    iter++;
    fprintf(stderr, "iter=%d size=%zu\n", iter, size);
    fflush(stderr);

    tlv_message *msg = NULL;
    if (tlv_parse(data, size, &msg) != 0) return 0;
    fprintf(stderr, "  msg=%p first_field=%p\n",
            (void*)msg,
            (msg && msg->n_fields) ? (void*)msg->field_data[0] : NULL);
    tlv_free(msg);
    return 0;
}
```

If you see the same pointer in `first_field` across iterations, suspect 2 is confirmed. If `msg` itself appears at the same address but `first_field` is fresh, it's allocator reuse — look at suspect 1.

### One more thing

While triaging this, also build with **MSan** (uninitialized memory) — but in a separate build because it doesn't compose with ASan. UAF often comes packaged with 'use of uninitialized' issues that MSan catches but ASan misses. Once you've fixed the static-state issue, an MSan run is a 10-minute investment that often turns up an extra two or three minor bugs.

Report the grep output and the iter logs, and we'll converge on which suspect it actually is.

Memoization is the top-down approach to dynamic programming: solve problems recursively, but cache results to avoid redundant computation. Instead of recalculating `fib(5)` every time it's needed, store the result after the first computation.

```python
@lru_cache(maxsize=None)
def fib(n):
    if n < 2: return n
    return fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)
# fib(100) is instant — each value computed once
```

The bottom-up alternative (tabulation) builds a table iteratively from base cases. Both achieve the same complexity but memoization is more intuitive while tabulation often uses less memory.

**JWT-based authentication flow:**

```python
from datetime import datetime, timedelta
import jwt
from fastapi import Depends, HTTPException
from fastapi.security import HTTPBearer, HTTPAuthorizationCredentials

SECRET = os.environ['JWT_SECRET']
ALGORITHM = 'HS256'

def create_token(user_id: int, expires_minutes: int = 15) -> str:
    payload = {
        'sub': str(user_id),
        'exp': datetime.utcnow() + timedelta(minutes=expires_minutes),
        'iat': datetime.utcnow(),
    }
    return jwt.encode(payload, SECRET, algorithm=ALGORITHM)

def create_refresh_token(user_id: int) -> str:
    return create_token(user_id, expires_minutes=60 * 24 * 7)  # 7 days

security = HTTPBearer()

async def get_current_user(
    credentials: HTTPAuthorizationCredentials = Depends(security),
) -> int:
    try:
        payload = jwt.decode(credentials.credentials, SECRET, algorithms=[ALGORITHM])
        return int(payload['sub'])
    except jwt.ExpiredSignatureError:
        raise HTTPException(401, 'Token expired')
    except jwt.InvalidTokenError:
        raise HTTPException(401, 'Invalid token')

# Login endpoint
@app.post('/auth/login')
async def login(email: str, password: str):
    user = await verify_credentials(email, password)
    if not user:
        raise HTTPException(401, 'Invalid credentials')
    return {
        'access_token': create_token(user.id),
        'refresh_token': create_refresh_token(user.id),
    }

# Protected endpoint
@app.get('/api/profile')
async def profile(user_id: int = Depends(get_current_user)):
    return await get_user(user_id)
```

**Security considerations:**
- Short-lived access tokens (15 min) + long-lived refresh tokens (7 days)
- Store refresh tokens in httpOnly cookies (not localStorage)
- Rotate refresh tokens on use (detect stolen tokens)
- Keep the JWT secret truly secret (env var, not in code)

`this` depends on **how** a function is called, not where it's defined.

```javascript
// 1. Method call → this = the object
const obj = {
  name: 'Alice',
  greet() { return this.name; }  // 'Alice'
};

// 2. Regular function call → this = undefined (strict) or window
function standalone() { return this; }  // undefined in strict mode

// 3. Arrow function → inherits this from enclosing scope
const obj2 = {
  name: 'Bob',
  greet: () => this.name,         // undefined! Arrow doesn't bind this
  greetCorrect() {
    const inner = () => this.name; // 'Bob' — inherits from greetCorrect
    return inner();
  }
};

// 4. Explicit binding
function say(greeting) { return `${greeting}, ${this.name}`; }
say.call({name: 'Charlie'}, 'Hi');    // 'Hi, Charlie'
say.bind({name: 'Dana'})('Hello');    // 'Hello, Dana'
```

Rule of thumb: use arrow functions for callbacks, regular functions for methods.

Three layers, in order of how much they prevent recurrence:

**Layer 1 — Single auth module, no escape hatch.** Make exactly one file in the codebase allowed to import `jsonwebtoken`. Everything else imports your wrapper, which exposes only `verifyToken` — no `decode`, no raw access. Enforce with an ESLint `no-restricted-imports` rule:

```js
// .eslintrc — fails CI if anything outside src/auth/ imports jsonwebtoken
module.exports = {
  overrides: [{
    files: ["src/**/*.ts"],
    excludedFiles: ["src/auth/**"],
    rules: {
      "no-restricted-imports": ["error", { paths: ["jsonwebtoken"] }],
    },
  }],
};
```

**Layer 2 — Branded types so "unverified claims" can't be passed where "verified claims" are expected.** This makes the bug a compile error:

```ts
// src/auth/index.ts
import jwt from "jsonwebtoken";

declare const _verified: unique symbol;
export type VerifiedClaims = {
  sub: string;
  iat: number;
  exp: number;
  readonly [_verified]: true; // brand — only verify can produce this
};

export function verifyToken(token: string): VerifiedClaims {
  const payload = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET!, {
    algorithms: ["HS256"],
    issuer: "your-app",
    audience: "your-app-api",
  }) as object;
  return payload as VerifiedClaims; // only this function does the cast
}

// src/auth/middleware.ts
import { Request, Response, NextFunction } from "express";
import { verifyToken, VerifiedClaims } from "./index";

declare module "express-serve-static-core" {
  interface Request { claims?: VerifiedClaims }
}

export function requireAuth(req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) {
  const header = req.get("authorization");
  if (!header?.startsWith("Bearer ")) return res.sendStatus(401);
  try {
    req.claims = verifyToken(header.slice(7));
    next();
  } catch {
    res.sendStatus(401);
  }
}
```

Now your `requireUser` takes `VerifiedClaims`, not a string or unbranded object. Anyone who tries to call `jwt.decode` and pass the result gets a TypeScript error. The previous bug becomes structurally impossible:

```ts
// src/users/loader.ts
export async function loadUserFromClaims(claims: VerifiedClaims): Promise<User> {
  return db.user.findUniqueOrThrow({ where: { id: claims.sub } });
}
```

**Layer 3 — Regression test that hits every protected route.** Even with all of the above, write the negative test once and you'll catch any future end-run:

```ts
import request from "supertest";
import { app } from "../src/app";
import { protectedRoutes } from "../src/routes"; // export the list

const forged = "eyJhbGciOiJub25lIn0.eyJzdWIiOiJhZG1pbiJ9.";

describe.each(protectedRoutes)("%s rejects alg=none", (method, path) => {
  it("returns 401", async () => {
    const res = await request(app)[method](path)
      .set("authorization", `Bearer ${forged}`);
    expect(res.status).toBe(401);
  });
});
```

If you grow into a multi-service architecture, Layer 4 is moving auth out of app code entirely — an Envoy/Kong/Istio JWT filter validates at the edge and forwards trusted claims as `x-claims-sub` headers that app code reads directly. Then no service can be wrong about JWT, because no service touches it.

For now, Layers 1 and 2 give you a defense that's enforced by the compiler and CI, not by reviewer attention. That's the real win: turning a class of bug into a syntax error.

Wrap each spawned future in `tokio::time::timeout` at the spawn site. The handler function doesn't need to know about the timeout:

```rust
use std::time::Duration;
use tokio::task::JoinSet;

const REQUEST_TIMEOUT: Duration = Duration::from_secs(60);
const SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT: Duration = Duration::from_secs(30);

// In the accept loop:
tasks.spawn(async move {
    match tokio::time::timeout(REQUEST_TIMEOUT, handle_connection(&pool, stream)).await {
        Ok(()) => {}
        Err(_elapsed) => {
            eprintln!("request to {addr} timed out after {REQUEST_TIMEOUT:?}");
        }
    }
});
```

When the timeout fires, Tokio drops the inner future. Dropping a future cancels it at its current `.await` point — the downstream HTTP client future gets dropped, which closes the TCP connection and frees all resources. This is Rust's structured concurrency advantage: cancellation is just `Drop`.

Here's the full server with both per-request and shutdown timeouts integrated:

```rust
use std::time::Duration;
use sqlx::PgPool;
use tokio::net::TcpListener;
use tokio::signal;
use tokio::task::JoinSet;

const REQUEST_TIMEOUT: Duration = Duration::from_secs(60);
const SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT: Duration = Duration::from_secs(30);

async fn handle_connection(pool: &PgPool, stream: tokio::net::TcpStream) {
    let _row = sqlx::query("SELECT 1").fetch_one(pool).await.unwrap();
    // ... handler logic, possibly with downstream HTTP calls
}

#[cfg(unix)]
async fn shutdown_signal() {
    let mut sigterm = signal::unix::signal(signal::unix::SignalKind::terminate())
        .expect("failed to install SIGTERM handler");
    tokio::select! {
        _ = signal::ctrl_c() => {}
        _ = sigterm.recv() => {}
    }
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    let pool = PgPool::connect("postgres://localhost/mydb")
        .await
        .unwrap();
    let listener = TcpListener::bind("0.0.0.0:8080").await.unwrap();
    let mut tasks = JoinSet::new();

    loop {
        tokio::select! {
            result = listener.accept() => {
                let (stream, addr) = result.unwrap();
                let pool = pool.clone();
                tasks.spawn(async move {
                    let handler = handle_connection(&pool, stream);
                    if tokio::time::timeout(REQUEST_TIMEOUT, handler).await.is_err() {
                        eprintln!("{addr}: request timed out after {REQUEST_TIMEOUT:?}");
                    }
                });
            }
            _ = shutdown_signal() => {
                eprintln!("shutting down, draining {} tasks", tasks.len());
                break;
            }
        }
        while let Some(result) = tasks.try_join_next() {
            if let Err(e) = result {
                eprintln!("task panicked: {e}");
            }
        }
    }

    let drain = async {
        while let Some(result) = tasks.join_next().await {
            if let Err(e) = result {
                eprintln!("task panicked during drain: {e}");
            }
        }
    };

    if tokio::time::timeout(SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT, drain).await.is_err() {
        eprintln!("{} tasks still running, aborting", tasks.len());
        tasks.abort_all();
    }

    pool.close().await;
    eprintln!("shutdown complete");
}
```

The two timeout layers work together: during normal operation, individual requests time out at 60 seconds. During shutdown, the drain waits up to 30 seconds for all remaining tasks (which themselves still have their individual timeouts running). If the drain times out, `abort_all()` forcibly cancels everything.

One caveat: cancellation safety. If `handle_connection` holds a lock or is in the middle of a multi-step database transaction when the timeout fires, dropping the future may leave things in an inconsistent state. If that's a concern, use `CancellationToken` for cooperative cancellation — the handler periodically checks the token and performs cleanup before exiting. But for most HTTP handlers that are just doing reads and writes, drop-based cancellation is fine.

The `Counter` class above already gives you per-instance locks, so two threads incrementing different tenants never contend. That's usually all you need.

Where it falls down: one *hot* tenant whose counter is being slammed by every thread. The fix is thread-local accumulation with periodic flush — trade read freshness for write throughput:

```python
import threading
from typing import ClassVar

class ShardedCounter:
    _local: ClassVar[threading.local] = threading.local()
    FLUSH_EVERY: ClassVar[int] = 64

    def __init__(self) -> None:
        self._value = 0
        self._lock = threading.Lock()

    def incr(self, n: int = 1) -> None:
        buf = getattr(self._local, "buf", None)
        if buf is None:
            buf = self._local.buf = {}
        pending = buf.get(id(self), 0) + n
        if pending >= self.FLUSH_EVERY:
            with self._lock:
                self._value += pending
            buf[id(self)] = 0
        else:
            buf[id(self)] = pending

    def flush(self) -> None:
        buf = getattr(self._local, "buf", {})
        pending = buf.pop(id(self), 0)
        if pending:
            with self._lock:
                self._value += pending

    @property
    def value(self) -> int:
        with self._lock:
            return self._value  # may be stale by up to FLUSH_EVERY * threads
```

Call `flush()` at thread shutdown (or wrap your worker loop in a `try/finally`). For an exact reading on demand, walk every active worker and flush — usually unnecessary; eventual consistency is fine for metrics-style counters.

If you ever migrate to free-threaded CPython (3.13t), revisit: lock-free atomic int support is landing, and the cost model changes. For now, on stock CPython, the per-instance lock with optional sharding is the boring correct answer.

Protocols (PEP 544, `typing.Protocol`) define structural subtyping — duck typing with static type checking. A class satisfies a Protocol if it has the right methods, without explicitly inheriting from it.

```python
from typing import Protocol, runtime_checkable

@runtime_checkable
class Drawable(Protocol):
    def draw(self, x: int, y: int) -> None: ...
    def get_bounds(self) -> tuple[int, int, int, int]: ...

class Circle:
    def draw(self, x: int, y: int) -> None:
        print(f"Drawing circle at ({x}, {y})")
    def get_bounds(self) -> tuple[int, int, int, int]:
        return (0, 0, 10, 10)

def render(shape: Drawable) -> None:
    shape.draw(0, 0)

render(Circle())  # Works — Circle satisfies Drawable structurally

# runtime_checkable enables isinstance checks:
isinstance(Circle(), Drawable)  # True
```

**When to use Protocols vs ABCs:**
- Use Protocol when you want to type-check third-party or built-in classes you can't modify
- Use Protocol when you want structural (duck) typing checked statically
- Use ABC when you want to enforce inheritance and provide shared implementation
- Protocols are the Pythonic equivalent of Go interfaces or TypeScript structural types

```javascript
// async/await — use try/catch
async function fetchUser(id) {
  try {
    const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
    return await res.json();
  } catch (error) {
    if (error.name === 'AbortError') {
      console.log('Request was cancelled');
    } else {
      console.error('Failed to fetch user:', error.message);
      throw error; // Re-throw if you can't handle it
    }
  }
}

// Promise chains — use .catch()
fetch('/api/data')
  .then(res => res.json())
  .then(data => process(data))
  .catch(err => console.error('Pipeline failed:', err));

// Global unhandled rejections (last resort)
window.addEventListener('unhandledrejection', (event) => {
  console.error('Unhandled promise rejection:', event.reason);
  event.preventDefault(); // Prevent default browser logging
});
```

Always handle errors at the right level. Don't catch if you can't do anything useful — let it bubble up to a centralized error handler.

Async state (loading, error, data, refetching) is the most common source of bugs in React apps. The pattern you choose depends on your app's complexity.

**Pattern 1: Custom hook (simple apps)**
```tsx
function useAsync<T>(asyncFn: () => Promise<T>, deps: unknown[]) {
    const [state, setState] = useState<{
        data: T | null;
        error: Error | null;
        loading: boolean;
    }>({ data: null, error: null, loading: true });

    useEffect(() => {
        let cancelled = false;
        setState(s => ({ ...s, loading: true, error: null }));

        asyncFn()
            .then(data => { if (!cancelled) setState({ data, error: null, loading: false }); })
            .catch(error => { if (!cancelled) setState({ data: null, error, loading: false }); });

        return () => { cancelled = true; };
    }, deps);

    return state;
}

// Usage
function UserProfile({ userId }: { userId: string }) {
    const { data: user, loading, error } = useAsync(
        () => api.getUser(userId), [userId]
    );

    if (loading) return <Skeleton />;
    if (error) return <ErrorBanner error={error} />;
    return <ProfileCard user={user!} />;
}
```

**Pattern 2: TanStack Query (most apps)**
```tsx
function UserProfile({ userId }: { userId: string }) {
    const { data: user, isLoading, error } = useQuery({
        queryKey: ['user', userId],
        queryFn: () => api.getUser(userId),
        staleTime: 5 * 60 * 1000,  // Cache for 5 minutes
    });
    // Handles caching, deduplication, background refetching,
    // retry, pagination, optimistic updates, prefetching
}
```

**Pattern 3: Zustand + async actions (when you need shared state)**
```tsx
const useUserStore = create<UserStore>((set) => ({
    users: {},
    loading: new Set<string>(),
    fetchUser: async (id: string) => {
        set(s => ({ loading: new Set(s.loading).add(id) }));
        try {
            const user = await api.getUser(id);
            set(s => {
                const loading = new Set(s.loading);
                loading.delete(id);
                return { users: { ...s.users, [id]: user }, loading };
            });
        } catch (error) {
            set(s => {
                const loading = new Set(s.loading);
                loading.delete(id);
                return { loading };
            });
            throw error;
        }
    },
}));
```

**Critical detail in all patterns:** The `cancelled` flag (or AbortController) in useEffect prevents setting state on unmounted components and handles race conditions when dependencies change faster than requests complete.

You're right that this breaks the rule, and the reason matters: real-time collab isn't really a state-management problem in the React sense. Trying to push CRDT updates through Zustand is fighting the tools.

**Split the problem into three layers:**

1. **Document state** — lives in a CRDT (Yjs, Automerge, Loro), *not* in React state. The CRDT has its own observation API and merges remote ops correctly. React just renders a view of it.
2. **Presence data** — cursors, selections, names. Use the CRDT lib's awareness/ephemeral channel; it's designed for high-frequency throwaway data that doesn't get persisted.
3. **Local UI state** — your normal Zustand-shaped concerns. Unchanged.

With Yjs that looks roughly like:

```tsx
import * as Y from 'yjs';
import { WebsocketProvider } from 'y-websocket';
import { useEffect, useState, useSyncExternalStore } from 'react';

const doc = new Y.Doc();
const provider = new WebsocketProvider('wss://...', 'room-id', doc);
const yText = doc.getText('content');

// Document content: subscribe via useSyncExternalStore so React doesn't tear
function useYText(yText: Y.Text): string {
  return useSyncExternalStore(
    (cb) => {
      yText.observe(cb);
      return () => yText.unobserve(cb);
    },
    () => yText.toString(),
    () => yText.toString(),
  );
}
```

**The key trick for cursors: per-user subscriptions, batched via rAF.**

A naive approach subscribes one component to "all presence" and re-renders the whole cursor layer on every update. That's the bottleneck you're worried about. Instead, render one component per remote user and have each subscribe only to its own slice:

```tsx
interface Presence {
  userId: string;
  name: string;
  cursor: { line: number; column: number } | null;
  color: string;
}

function useRemoteCursor(userId: string): Presence | null {
  const [presence, setPresence] = useState<Presence | null>(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const awareness = provider.awareness;
    let frame = 0;
    let pending: Presence | null = null;

    const flush = () => {
      frame = 0;
      setPresence(pending);
    };

    const onChange = () => {
      const states = awareness.getStates();
      const next = (states.get(parseInt(userId)) as Presence | undefined) ?? null;
      pending = next;
      if (frame === 0) frame = requestAnimationFrame(flush);
    };

    awareness.on('change', onChange);
    onChange();
    return () => {
      awareness.off('change', onChange);
      if (frame !== 0) cancelAnimationFrame(frame);
    };
  }, [userId]);

  return presence;
}
```

Two things doing work here:
- Each cursor component owns its own subscription, so a single user moving only re-renders that user's `<RemoteCursor />`.
- The rAF coalescing means you render at most 60 times per second per cursor regardless of WebSocket message rate.

**What stays in Zustand:** "is the comment panel open," "current selection mode," "document title in the header." Low frequency, cross-cutting — exactly the bucket from before.

**What you should measure:** open the React Profiler with the collab session running and 5-10 simulated peers. If a cursor render takes <1ms and isn't triggering parent re-renders, you're fine. If you see the editor body re-rendering on cursor moves, you've still got a wiring bug — usually a context provider higher up that's swallowing the update.

The React Compiler (shipped experimentally with React 19) auto-inserts memoization at build time. Here's what it changes and what it doesn't:

**What the compiler does:**
```jsx
// You write this (no manual memoization):
function Dashboard({ user }) {
  const stats = calculateExpensiveStats(user.transactions);
  const handleExport = () => exportToCSV(stats);
  return <StatsPanel stats={stats} onExport={handleExport} />;
}

// The compiler transforms it to roughly:
function Dashboard({ user }) {
  const stats = useMemo(() => calculateExpensiveStats(user.transactions), [user.transactions]);
  const handleExport = useCallback(() => exportToCSV(stats), [stats]);
  return <StatsPanel stats={stats} onExport={handleExport} />;
}
```

It analyzes which values depend on which, and caches everything that doesn't need to change. You stop writing `useMemo` and `useCallback` by hand.

**What it does NOT do:**
```jsx
// 1. It can't fix algorithmic problems
const stats = calculateExpensiveStats(allTransactionsEver);
// Compiler memoizes it, but if the input changes every render, it still runs.
// You still need to think about WHAT data you pass, not just caching.

// 2. It can't optimize across component boundaries you control
// If a parent passes a new object literal every render:
<Dashboard user={{ ...user, lastSeen: Date.now() }} />
// Every render creates a new user object → all memoization inside Dashboard
// busts because the input reference changed. Compiler can't fix the parent.

// 3. It requires code to follow the Rules of Hooks
// Conditional hooks, mutating props, etc. break the compiler's analysis.
```

**Practical advice today:**
- If you're on React 19 and can enable the compiler, do it. Remove most manual `useMemo`/`useCallback` and let it handle things.
- Keep manual memoization for cases where you've profiled a specific bottleneck and want explicit control.
- Understanding *why* memoization works is still essential for debugging. When something is still slow despite the compiler, you need to know where to look.

The compiler makes useMemo largely unnecessary to *write*, but not unnecessary to *understand*.

Yes, and the canonical case is when the closure's *inputs* or *outputs* need to borrow from something the caller has already named. HRTB says "any lifetime" — sometimes you explicitly want "*this* lifetime, the one I've already got."

```rust
// The caller owns a dictionary. The closure looks keys up in it.
// The OUTPUT &str must borrow from the dictionary, not from the input key.
fn translate<'a, F>(keys: &[&str], lookup: F) -> Vec<&'a str>
where
    F: Fn(&str) -> &'a str,  // 'a is tied to dictionary lifetime, not free
{
    keys.iter().map(|k| lookup(k)).collect()
}

fn main() {
    let dict: Vec<(&str, &str)> = vec![("hi", "hello"), ("bye", "goodbye")];
    let lookup = |k: &str| -> &str {
        dict.iter().find(|(src, _)| *src == k).map(|(_, dst)| *dst).unwrap_or("?")
    };

    let out: Vec<&str> = translate(&["hi", "bye"], lookup);
    println!("{:?}", out);
}
```

Here `'a` is the lifetime of the `&str`s inside `dict`. The return type `Vec<&'a str>` is tied to that specific lifetime. If you made the bound higher-ranked — `for<'b> Fn(&'b str) -> &'b str` — you'd be saying the output borrows from the *input key* (same lifetime as `&str` passed in), which is a different, stricter constraint and wrong for this API.

The general rule: when the closure's output borrows from something the closure *captures* (not from its inputs), you need a rank-1 lifetime naming that captured thing. When the closure's output borrows from its inputs regardless of which input, HRTB is correct. HRTB is wrong when it accidentally hides a real lifetime relationship — and rank-1 exists to let you express that relationship.

Overrides are sneaky precisely because the diff looks small (one line in package.json) but the lockfile blast radius can be large. Three concrete defenses:

**Defense 1: Flag overrides changes in CI.**

```yaml
# .github/workflows/overrides-review.yml
on: [pull_request]
jobs:
  flag-overrides:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
        with: { fetch-depth: 0 }
      - name: Detect override changes
        run: |
          if git diff origin/main HEAD -- package.json | grep -E '"(overrides|patchedDependencies|packageExtensions)"'; then
            echo "::warning::This PR modifies pnpm overrides/patches. Requires platform-team review."
            echo "REQUIRES_REVIEW=true" >> $GITHUB_ENV
          fi
      - uses: actions/labeler@v5
        if: env.REQUIRES_REVIEW == 'true'
        with: { configuration-path: .github/override-label.yml }
```

Pair with CODEOWNERS:

```
# CODEOWNERS
package.json @org/platform-team
pnpm-lock.yaml @org/platform-team
```

**Defense 2: Resolution diff in PR comments.** Generate a human-readable diff of resolved versions:

```javascript
// scripts/lockfile-diff.mjs — run via `node scripts/lockfile-diff.mjs main HEAD`
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { execSync } from 'node:child_process';
import YAML from 'yaml';

const [base, head] = process.argv.slice(2);

function loadLockfile(ref) {
  const content = execSync(`git show ${ref}:pnpm-lock.yaml`, { encoding: 'utf8' });
  return YAML.parse(content);
}

function extractPackages(lockfile) {
  const out = new Map();
  for (const [key, entry] of Object.entries(lockfile.packages || {})) {
    // key looks like '/lodash@4.17.21' or '/@scope/pkg@1.2.3(peer@x.y.z)'
    const match = key.match(/^\/(@[^/]+\/[^@]+|[^@]+)@([^()]+)/);
    if (match) {
      const [, name, version] = match;
      if (!out.has(name)) out.set(name, new Set());
      out.get(name).add(version);
    }
  }
  return out;
}

const before = extractPackages(loadLockfile(base));
const after = extractPackages(loadLockfile(head));

const names = new Set([...before.keys(), ...after.keys()]);
for (const name of [...names].sort()) {
  const b = [...(before.get(name) || [])].sort();
  const a = [...(after.get(name) || [])].sort();
  if (JSON.stringify(b) !== JSON.stringify(a)) {
    console.log(`${name}:  ${b.join(', ') || '(none)'}  →  ${a.join(', ') || '(none)'}`);
  }
}
```

Wire that into a PR comment. When someone adds `"react": "18.3.1"` to overrides, the comment shows every package whose resolved react version changed. Reviewers can't miss it.

**Defense 3: `pnpm why` as part of override-PR template.** Make adding an override require filling out:

```
## Override justification
- Package: <name>
- Forced version: <version>
- Reason: <bug fix / security CVE / pinning>
- Output of `pnpm why <name>`:
  ```
  <paste here>
  ```
```

This is process, not tooling, but it forces the override author to confront the blast radius before merge.

One last thing: pnpm 9 added `pnpm.allowedDeprecatedVersions` and `pnpm.peerDependencyRules.allowedVersions` which can sometimes replace overrides with narrower scope. If your override is just "silence the peer dep warning," use `peerDependencyRules` instead — it's strictly less powerful and therefore less risky.

```css
/* Mobile-first approach (start with mobile, add complexity) */
.container {
  padding: 16px;
}

/* Tablet and up */
@media (min-width: 768px) {
  .container {
    padding: 24px;
