Flexbox is for one-dimensional layouts (row OR column). Use Grid for two-dimensional layouts.

That is a real and important race condition. The standard solution is **worker heartbeats**: the worker periodically touches the job's timestamp while processing, and `recoverStale` only recovers jobs whose heartbeat has gone stale.

```javascript
class Worker {
  constructor(queue, handler, { heartbeatMs = 5000 } = {}) {
    this.queue = queue;
    this.handler = handler;
    this.heartbeatMs = heartbeatMs;
    this.running = false;
  }

  async start() {
    this.running = true;
    this._maintenanceInterval = setInterval(async () => {
      try {
        await this.queue.promoteDelayed();
        await this.queue.recoverStale();
      } catch (err) {
        console.error('Maintenance error:', err);
      }
    }, 1000);

    while (this.running) {
      try {
        const job = await this.queue.claim(5);
        if (!job) continue;

        // Start heartbeat — keeps the job "fresh" in recoverStale's eyes
        const heartbeat = setInterval(async () => {
          try {
            await this.queue.redis.hset(
              `${this.queue.keys.job}:${job.id}`,
              'updatedAt', Date.now(),
            );
          } catch (err) {
            console.error(`Heartbeat failed for ${job.id}:`, err);
          }
        }, this.heartbeatMs);

        try {
          await this.handler(job);
          await this.queue.complete(job.id);
        } catch (err) {
          console.error(`Job ${job.id} failed:`, err.message);
          await this.queue.retry(job.id, err);
        } finally {
          clearInterval(heartbeat);  // always stop heartbeat
        }
      } catch (err) {
        console.error('Worker error:', err);
        await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 1000));
      }
    }
  }

  async stop() {
    this.running = false;
    clearInterval(this._maintenanceInterval);
  }
}
```

**Now set `staleAfterMs` to be significantly longer than the heartbeat interval:**

```javascript
// Heartbeat every 5 seconds, recover after 30 seconds of no heartbeat
const worker = new Worker(queue, processJob, { heartbeatMs: 5000 });

// In recoverStale, default staleAfterMs = 30_000
// A living worker heartbeats every 5s → updatedAt is always fresh
// A dead worker stops heartbeating → after 30s, job is recovered
```

**Timeline for a 45-second job with a living worker:**
```
t=0s:  Worker claims job, starts processing, starts heartbeat
t=5s:  Heartbeat → updatedAt = now
t=10s: Heartbeat → updatedAt = now
t=15s: recoverStale runs → job age = 5s < 30s → not stale ✓
t=20s: Heartbeat → updatedAt = now
t=25s: Heartbeat → updatedAt = now
t=30s: recoverStale runs → job age = 5s < 30s → not stale ✓
t=45s: Worker finishes → complete() → heartbeat stopped
```

**Timeline for a crashed worker:**
```
t=0s:  Worker claims job, starts processing, starts heartbeat
t=5s:  Heartbeat → updatedAt = now
t=7s:  *** WORKER CRASHES *** → heartbeat stops
t=15s: recoverStale runs → job age = 8s < 30s → not yet stale
t=30s: recoverStale runs → job age = 23s < 30s → not yet stale
t=37s: recoverStale runs → job age = 30s >= 30s → RECOVERED ✓
       Job moves back to pending, another worker picks it up
```

**The remaining edge case — and why you need idempotent handlers:**

There is still a tiny window where duplicate processing can occur. Imagine:
1. Worker A's heartbeat fails (network blip, not a crash)
2. `recoverStale` moves the job back to pending
3. Worker B claims and starts processing it
4. Worker A's network recovers, it finishes and calls `complete()`

Now both A and B processed the same job. This is inherent to at-least-once queues — it is impossible to guarantee exactly-once processing in a distributed system without making the handler idempotent.

**Make your handlers idempotent:**
```javascript
async function processJob(job) {
  // Idempotency key: use the job ID as a deduplication check
  const alreadyDone = await db.query(
    'SELECT 1 FROM processed_jobs WHERE job_id = $1',
    [job.id]
  );
  if (alreadyDone.rows.length > 0) return;  // already processed

  // ... do the actual work ...

  // Record completion atomically with the work
  await db.query(
    'INSERT INTO processed_jobs (job_id, completed_at) VALUES ($1, NOW())',
    [job.id]
  );
}
```

This is the fundamental pattern: the queue guarantees **at-least-once delivery**, and the handler guarantees **idempotent processing**. Together, they give you effectively-once semantics.

```typescript
import { useEffect, useRef, useCallback } from 'react';

function useInfiniteScroll(loadMore: () => void, hasMore: boolean) {
  const observer = useRef<IntersectionObserver>();

  const lastElementRef = useCallback((node: HTMLElement | null) => {
    if (observer.current) observer.current.disconnect();
    if (!hasMore) return;

    observer.current = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
      if (entries[0].isIntersecting) {
        loadMore();
      }
    });

    if (node) observer.current.observe(node);
  }, [loadMore, hasMore]);

  return lastElementRef;
}

// Usage
function ItemList() {
  const [items, setItems] = useState<Item[]>([]);
  const [page, setPage] = useState(1);
  const [hasMore, setHasMore] = useState(true);

  const loadMore = useCallback(async () => {
    const newItems = await fetchItems(page);
    setItems(prev => [...prev, ...newItems]);
    setHasMore(newItems.length === 20);
    setPage(p => p + 1);
  }, [page]);

  const lastRef = useInfiniteScroll(loadMore, hasMore);

  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map((item, i) => (
        <li key={item.id} ref={i === items.length - 1 ? lastRef : undefined}>
          {item.name}
        </li>
      ))}
      {!hasMore && <li>No more items</li>}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

`IntersectionObserver` is more performant than scroll event listeners — it's handled by the browser's rendering engine, not JavaScript.

No, as-written it's racy, and you spotted the exact problem. Let me break down why, because "shared_ptr is thread-safe" is one of the most dangerous half-truths in C++ concurrency.

**What IS safe:** the control block's reference count. If two threads each hold their own independent `shared_ptr<Config>` instance that happens to point at the same Config, they can copy, destroy, or reset their own instances concurrently. The refcount increments and decrements are atomic.

**What is NOT safe:** concurrent access to the same shared_ptr instance where at least one thread writes. Your `cfg_` member is a single shared_ptr. When `refresh()` does `cfg_ = std::make_shared<const Config>()` and simultaneously `get()` reads `cfg_`, you have a data race on that instance.

Mechanically: a shared_ptr is two pointers internally — the raw pointer to the managed object, and a pointer to the control block. Assignment updates both, plus it manipulates refcounts. While each individual refcount operation is atomic, the pair of pointer writes is not atomic with respect to reads. A reader can observe a torn state: new raw pointer with old control block, or new control block with old raw pointer. TSan flags exactly this:

```
WARNING: ThreadSanitizer: data race
  Write of size 8 at 0x7b0c... by thread T2:
    #0 std::__shared_ptr<Config>::operator=(...) 
    #1 ConfigCache::refresh()
  Previous read of size 8 at 0x7b0c... by thread T1:
    #0 std::__shared_ptr<Config>::__shared_ptr(shared_ptr const&)
    #1 ConfigCache::get() const
```

**The fix (C++20):** `std::atomic<std::shared_ptr<const Config>>`.

```cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <memory>

class ConfigCache {
public:
    ConfigCache() : cfg_(std::make_shared<const Config>()) {}

    // Readers: each gets a private snapshot.
    std::shared_ptr<const Config> snapshot() const {
        return cfg_.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
    }

    // Writer: atomically publishes the new config.
    void refresh() {
        cfg_.store(std::make_shared<const Config>(),
                   std::memory_order_release);
    }

private:
    std::atomic<std::shared_ptr<const Config>> cfg_;
};
```

Two things make this correct:

1. `atomic<shared_ptr<T>>::load/store` swaps the raw-pointer + control-block pair as a unit. No torn reads.
2. `shared_ptr<const Config>` — the Config is immutable after construction. Readers operate on their snapshot; nobody mutates the pointee. Refresh publishes a brand new Config rather than editing the existing one.

I removed the `get() -> const Config&` accessor deliberately. Returning a reference lets a reader hang onto it past the snapshot's lifetime:

```cpp
const Config& c = cache.get();  // snapshot's refcount bumped and dropped immediately
// another thread calls refresh() — the Config c refers to may be destroyed
use(c.endpoint);  // use-after-free
```

Force readers to hold the shared_ptr for the duration they care about:

```cpp
auto snap = cache.snapshot();   // shared_ptr; keeps Config alive
use(snap->endpoint);
use(snap->timeout_ms);
// snap drops when it goes out of scope; if it was the last holder of the old
// config (because refresh happened), the old Config is destroyed here.
```

**Pre-C++20 migration note.** If you're stuck on C++17, use the deprecated-but-functional free functions: `std::atomic_load(&cfg_)`, `std::atomic_store(&cfg_, new_sp)`. These take a `std::shared_ptr<T>*` and do the same job. They're deprecated in C++20 and removed in C++26, so treat them as a migration path, not a destination.

**Performance.** `std::atomic<std::shared_ptr<T>>` is usually implemented with an internal lock (libstdc++ uses a spinlock in a globally-hashed lock table keyed on the atomic's address). It is not wait-free. For your use case — infrequent writes, many reads, each read doing an atomic load + shared_ptr copy — the lock window is a few instructions long and effectively uncontended, so it's performance-indistinguishable from lock-free in practice. Only reach for something more exotic (folly hazptr, RCU) if a profiler tells you the atomic<shared_ptr> load is actually hot, which it almost never is for config-style data.

The two-line rule for this kind of cache:
1. If the pointer can change, guard the pointer with `atomic<shared_ptr<T>>`.
2. Make the pointee const so you don't have to guard its contents too.

Not a hard rule — a default. The principle is: don't put the form *engine* (field values, dirty/touched, validation, error state, registration) in your global store. The form library has optimized internals — uncontrolled inputs, refs, subscriptions per field — that you'd be reimplementing badly if you reduced everything through a store on every keystroke.

But *projecting* specific values from the form to other parts of the UI is fine, and your case is the legitimate reason to do it. Two patterns, depending on tree topology:

**Same React subtree (sidebar preview is rendered inside the page that holds the form):** use React Hook Form's `FormProvider` and `useWatch`. The preview subscribes to specific fields and only re-renders when those fields change.

```tsx
<FormProvider {...methods}>
  <ProductForm />
  <ProductPreview /> {/* subscribes via useWatch */}
</FormProvider>

function ProductPreview() {
  const title = useWatch({ name: 'title' });
  const price = useWatch({ name: 'price' });
  // re-renders only when title or price change, not on every keystroke
  return <div>...</div>;
}
```

This keeps form internals scoped, gives you fine-grained reactivity for free, and avoids storing transient typing state globally.

**Different subtrees (preview is in a header, sidebar, or completely separate part of the layout):** project to Zustand, but only the values you actually need, ideally debounced.

```tsx
// store
const usePreviewState = create<{title: string; price: number}>(() => ({
  title: '',
  price: 0,
}));

// inside the form
const { watch } = useFormContext();
useEffect(() => {
  const subscription = watch((values) => {
    usePreviewState.setState({
      title: values.title ?? '',
      price: values.price ?? 0,
    });
  });
  return () => subscription.unsubscribe();
}, [watch]);

// in the unrelated component
function HeaderPreview() {
  const title = usePreviewState((s) => s.title);
  return <h1>{title}</h1>;
}
```

Couple of refinements worth knowing:

- **Debounce if the preview is expensive.** A markdown preview that re-parses on every keystroke is painful; wrap the watch handler in a debounced setter (`debounce(set, 100)`).
- **Don't store the entire form** — only the fields the preview reads. The full form state is the form's job.
- **Reset on unmount.** When the form unmounts, clear the preview slice or your header will show stale data on the next page.

The refined rule: **the form library owns the form's working state; the global store can hold projections of that state for other UI to read.** That separation is what the default advice is protecting — putting all form internals in the store is what creates the re-render storms and the validation tangles, not the projection itself.

Browser-native custom HTML elements with encapsulated styles and behavior. Three main technologies:

1. **Custom Elements:** Define new HTML tags with JavaScript classes.
2. **Shadow DOM:** Encapsulated DOM and CSS that doesn't leak in or out.
3. **HTML Templates:** Inert markup instantiated via JavaScript.

```javascript
class MyCard extends HTMLElement {
    constructor() {
        super();
        const shadow = this.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });
        shadow.innerHTML = `
            <style>
                .card { border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 16px; border-radius: 8px; }
            </style>
            <div class="card">
                <slot></slot>
            </div>
        `;
    }
}
customElements.define('my-card', MyCard);

// Usage in HTML:
// <my-card>Hello World</my-card>
```

Benefits: framework-agnostic (work with React, Vue, Angular, or plain HTML), native browser support, true style encapsulation. Drawbacks: more verbose than framework components, limited ecosystem compared to React/Vue.

Your version is a single object with optional fields, so `ok: boolean` doesn't narrow the other fields. You need a **discriminated union** where `ok` is the discriminant literal:

```typescript
export type Result<T, E = unknown> =
  | { readonly ok: true; readonly data: T }
  | { readonly ok: false; readonly error: E };

// Constructors — use these instead of object literals
export const ok = <T>(data: T): Result<T, never> => ({ ok: true, data });
export const err = <E>(error: E): Result<never, E> => ({ ok: false, error });

// Map success, leave failure untouched
export function map<T, U, E>(
  result: Result<T, E>,
  fn: (value: T) => U
): Result<U, E> {
  return result.ok ? ok(fn(result.data)) : result;
}

// Map error, leave success untouched
export function mapErr<T, E, F>(
  result: Result<T, E>,
  fn: (error: E) => F
): Result<T, F> {
  return result.ok ? result : err(fn(result.error));
}

// Chain — like flatMap, lets the function itself fail
export function andThen<T, U, E>(
  result: Result<T, E>,
  fn: (value: T) => Result<U, E>
): Result<U, E> {
  return result.ok ? fn(result.data) : result;
}

export function unwrapOr<T, E>(result: Result<T, E>, fallback: T): T {
  return result.ok ? result.data : fallback;
}

// Exhaustive match
export function match<T, E, R>(
  result: Result<T, E>,
  branches: { ok: (value: T) => R; err: (error: E) => R }
): R {
  return result.ok ? branches.ok(result.data) : branches.err(result.error);
}
```

Usage demonstrating that narrowing works:

```typescript
type ApiError = { code: number; message: string };

async function getUser(id: string): Promise<Result<{ name: string }, ApiError>> {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
  if (!res.ok) return err({ code: res.status, message: res.statusText });
  return ok(await res.json());
}

const r = await getUser("42");

if (r.ok) {
  console.log(r.data.name);   // ✅ data is { name: string }, not optional
  // r.error                   // ❌ TS error: doesn't exist on this branch
} else {
  console.log(r.error.code);  // ✅ error is ApiError, not optional
}

const upper = map(r, u => u.name.toUpperCase()); // Result<string, ApiError>
```

Key points:
- `readonly` on the fields prevents accidental mutation — Results should be immutable values.
- `ok` returns `Result<T, never>` and `err` returns `Result<never, E>` so they're assignable to any compatible Result type without casts (`never` is the bottom type — assignable to everything).
- Default `E = unknown` is safer than `E = Error` because not every error is an `Error` instance (string errors, structured errors, network errors).
- `andThen` is what you'll want for chaining fallible operations: `andThen(getUser(id), u => getOrders(u.id))`.

Avoid the `class Result` approach you'll see in some libraries — it doesn't serialize across worker/network boundaries and instanceof checks break across realms. Plain objects with discriminants serialize cleanly to JSON and work everywhere.

Fair — let me put it concretely. Clock drift compensation lives **outside** the per-packet hot path, in a slow control loop that nudges your playout rate.

**The setup.** Sender's audio is sampled at notionally 48000 Hz, but its actual hardware rate is `48000 * (1 + ε_s)` where `ε_s` is a few ppm offset. Receiver's playback DAC is `48000 * (1 + ε_r)`. The relative drift `ε_s - ε_r` is typically 10-200 ppm — meaning over 10 minutes you accumulate 6-125 ms of error. Definitely audible. Without compensation, your buffer slowly fills to overflow or drains to underrun.

**How to detect drift.**

The insight: if there were no network jitter, `arrival_time(i) - send_time(i)` would be constant. Network jitter is asymmetric — packets can arrive late but never "early" (faster than the network minimum). So the **minimum** of `(arrival_time - rtp_send_time)` over a window represents the floor. The slope of that floor over wall-clock time *is* the drift.

```go
type DriftTracker struct {
    mu sync.Mutex
    // Convert RTP timestamp to a wall-clock-equivalent local time:
    // localEquiv = arrivalTime - (rtpTs - firstRtpTs)/sampleRate
    // Then min(localEquiv) over a window represents the no-jitter baseline.
    firstArrival time.Time
    firstRtpTs   uint32
    sampleRate   float64

    windowSec    float64        // e.g. 5.0
    windowMin    time.Duration  // running minimum over the current window
    windowStart  time.Time
    prevWindowMin time.Duration
    prevWindowStart time.Time

    driftPpm     float64        // current estimate, parts per million
}

func (d *DriftTracker) OnPacket(arrival time.Time, rtpTs uint32) {
    d.mu.Lock()
    defer d.mu.Unlock()

    if d.firstArrival.IsZero() {
        d.firstArrival = arrival
        d.firstRtpTs = rtpTs
        d.windowStart = arrival
        d.windowMin = 0
        return
    }

    rtpElapsed := time.Duration(float64(int32(rtpTs - d.firstRtpTs)) / d.sampleRate * float64(time.Second))
    arrivalElapsed := arrival.Sub(d.firstArrival)
    skew := arrivalElapsed - rtpElapsed   // grows linearly with drift, modulo jitter

    if skew < d.windowMin || arrival.Sub(d.windowStart) < 100*time.Millisecond {
        d.windowMin = skew
    }

    if arrival.Sub(d.windowStart) > time.Duration(d.windowSec * float64(time.Second)) {
        if !d.prevWindowStart.IsZero() {
            dt := arrival.Sub(d.prevWindowStart).Seconds()
            if dt > 0 {
                slopeSec := (d.windowMin - d.prevWindowMin).Seconds() / dt
                d.driftPpm = slopeSec * 1e6
            }
        }
        d.prevWindowMin = d.windowMin
        d.prevWindowStart = d.windowStart
        d.windowStart = arrival
        d.windowMin = skew
    }
}

func (d *DriftTracker) DriftPpm() float64 {
    d.mu.Lock()
    defer d.mu.Unlock()
    return d.driftPpm
}
```

**How you apply the correction.**

The drift estimate updates every ~5 seconds. Positive `driftPpm` means the receiver's clock is slow relative to the sender — receiver is consuming samples slower than they arrive, buffer grows. You need to **drain faster** by playing slightly faster than nominal. Negative drift, the opposite.

The playout rate correction is tiny: at 200 ppm, you're playing at 1.0002x. You don't do this with WSOLA every frame (overkill, adds CPU); you do it by **skipping or inserting one frame per N frames during silence**. Specifically:

```go
// In the playout thread, every silence frame:
driftSamplesPerFrame := jb.driftTracker.DriftPpm() * 1e-6 * float64(framesPerPacket)
buf.driftAccum += driftSamplesPerFrame
if buf.driftAccum >= float64(framesPerPacket) {
    // Receiver clock too slow, skip a silence frame to catch up.
    skipNextSilenceFrame()
    buf.driftAccum -= float64(framesPerPacket)
} else if buf.driftAccum <= -float64(framesPerPacket) {
    // Receiver clock too fast, insert a silence frame.
    insertSilenceFrame()
    buf.driftAccum += float64(framesPerPacket)
}
```

During active speech, you *could* use WSOLA for the same correction, but on a multi-minute timescale the silence-frame approach is enough — most calls have plenty of silence. NetEQ is more aggressive and applies the correction continuously via its WSOLA, which is why it sounds smoother on calls with very little silence.

**Sanity-check:** clamp `driftPpm` to a reasonable range (e.g. ±500 ppm) so a transient network event can't trick you into a runaway correction. And if you ever see drift > 1000 ppm, something is wrong with your timestamp arithmetic, not with the clocks.

That's the whole story. The control loop is slow (5-second windows), the correction is tiny per frame, and the user never hears it because you only act during silence.

The magic-scan handles random garbage, but there are two resilience gaps to close:

**1. CRC failure should resync, not abort.** Currently a CRC mismatch returns an error that propagates to the caller and potentially kills the connection. Instead, skip the false-positive magic bytes and keep scanning:

```rust
impl Decoder for PacketCodec {
    type Item = Packet;
    type Error = io::Error;

    fn decode(&mut self, buf: &mut BytesMut) -> Result<Option<Packet>, io::Error> {
        loop {
            if buf.len() < HEADER_LEN {
                return Ok(None);
            }

            let magic = u16::from_be_bytes([buf[0], buf[1]]);
            if magic != MAGIC {
                buf.advance(1);
                continue;
            }

            let payload_len = u16::from_be_bytes([buf[4], buf[5]]);
            if payload_len > self.max_payload {
                // Corrupted length — skip this magic and rescan
                buf.advance(2);
                continue;
            }

            let total_len = HEADER_LEN + payload_len as usize + CHECKSUM_LEN;
            if buf.len() < total_len {
                return Ok(None);
            }

            // Peek at the data without consuming yet
            let computed_crc = crc16_ccitt(&buf[..total_len - CHECKSUM_LEN]);
            let received_crc = u16::from_be_bytes([
                buf[total_len - 2],
                buf[total_len - 1],
            ]);

            if computed_crc != received_crc {
                // CRC mismatch — this was a false magic match.
                // Skip past the magic bytes and keep scanning.
                buf.advance(2);
                continue;
            }

            // CRC valid — consume the frame
            let raw = buf.split_to(total_len);
            return Ok(Some(Packet {
                version: raw[2],
                msg_type: raw[3],
                payload: raw[HEADER_LEN..HEADER_LEN + payload_len as usize].to_vec(),
            }));
        }
    }
}
```

**Key change:** the CRC check happens *before* `split_to`. If the CRC fails, we advance past the false magic bytes and loop to scan for the next valid packet. No error returned, no data lost.

**2. Bound the resync search.** If the buffer fills with megabytes of garbage (a misbehaving sensor flooding the connection), the scan loop could spin for a long time. Add a limit:

```rust
const MAX_SCAN_BYTES: usize = 65536;

// At the top of the decode loop:
if buf.len() > MAX_SCAN_BYTES {
    // Too much unrecognized data — drain it and wait for fresh data
    let drain = buf.len() - HEADER_LEN;
    buf.advance(drain);
}
```

This caps CPU spent on resync. If 64KB of data doesn't contain a valid packet, the stream is probably too corrupted to recover inline — drain it and hope the next read brings cleaner data.

With these two changes, the parser survives garbage injection, partial corruption, and sensors that disconnect mid-packet. Valid packets that follow corrupted data are recovered automatically.

Your tech lead is right. A 6-7x gap between two hash functions in the same JVM almost always means one got to C2 (the optimizing tier) and the other didn't, not that one is genuinely faster. You need to show him JMH numbers.

**What happens during warmup, concretely.** HotSpot runs tiered compilation: your method starts in the interpreter, moves to C1 (fast compile, moderate codegen) after ~1500 invocations, then to C2 (slow compile, aggressive codegen) after ~10000. Graal is similar but more aggressive. Each transition is a cliff. For a hashing microbenchmark you typically see:

```
Iteration 1:  2000 ns/op  (interpreted)
Iteration 2:  180  ns/op  (C1 kicked in)
Iteration 3:  45   ns/op  (C2 kicked in)
Iteration 4:  12   ns/op  (loop unrolled, inlining complete, escape analysis done)
Iteration 5:  12   ns/op  (steady state)
```

If your 80 ns/op measurement caught strategy A while it was still in C1 and your 12 ns/op measurement caught strategy B after C2, you've measured the JIT, not the algorithm.

**What JMH does for you.** JMH is effectively the only credible way to measure JVM microbenchmarks:

- Forks a fresh JVM (no class-loading pollution from your IDE, no AOT cache crosstalk)
- Runs configurable warmup iterations that are discarded
- Uses `Blackhole` to consume results and block dead-code elimination
- Handles on-stack replacement correctly
- Computes confidence intervals
- Can dump perf counters and profile allocation / lock contention

Here's your benchmark done right:

```kotlin
// build.gradle.kts
plugins { id("me.champeau.jmh") version "0.7.2" }
dependencies {
    jmh("org.openjdk.jmh:jmh-core:1.37")
    jmh("org.openjdk.jmh:jmh-generator-annprocess:1.37")
}

// src/jmh/kotlin/HashBench.kt
import org.openjdk.jmh.annotations.*
import org.openjdk.jmh.infra.Blackhole
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit

@BenchmarkMode(Mode.AverageTime)
@OutputTimeUnit(TimeUnit.NANOSECONDS)
@State(Scope.Benchmark)
@Warmup(iterations = 10, time = 1, timeUnit = TimeUnit.SECONDS)
@Measurement(iterations = 10, time = 1, timeUnit = TimeUnit.SECONDS)
@Fork(value = 3, jvmArgs = ["-Xms2g", "-Xmx2g"])
open class HashBench {
    @Param("8", "64", "512")
    var size: Int = 0

    private lateinit var input: ByteArray

    @Setup
    fun setup() {
        input = ByteArray(size).also { java.util.Random(42).nextBytes(it) }
    }

    @Benchmark
    fun strategyA(bh: Blackhole) {
        bh.consume(strategyAHash(input))
    }

    @Benchmark
    fun strategyB(bh: Blackhole) {
        bh.consume(strategyBHash(input))
    }
}
```

Run with `./gradlew jmh`. You'll get output like:

```
Benchmark              (size)  Mode  Cnt   Score   Error  Units
HashBench.strategyA         8  avgt   30  14.2 ± 0.3   ns/op
HashBench.strategyA        64  avgt   30  22.1 ± 0.4   ns/op
HashBench.strategyA       512  avgt   30  89.3 ± 1.2   ns/op
HashBench.strategyB         8  avgt   30  13.8 ± 0.3   ns/op
HashBench.strategyB        64  avgt   30  21.4 ± 0.5   ns/op
HashBench.strategyB       512  avgt   30  85.1 ± 1.0   ns/op
```

**My prediction for your case:** Once warmed up, the gap will shrink dramatically. Maybe A is 1.1-1.3x slower than B, maybe they're indistinguishable within the error bars. The 6.7x gap you saw is almost certainly a warmup artifact. If the gap persists at 3 forks and 10 iterations, *then* it's real and worth a deeper look (maybe A has a branch that deoptimizes the JIT, or allocates, or uses a method that doesn't inline).

**The other big JIT effect to understand: escape analysis and scalar replacement.** If your hash strategy allocates a temporary buffer:

```kotlin
fun strategyAHash(input: ByteArray): Long {
    val buf = ByteBuffer.allocate(16)  // looks expensive
    buf.put(input, 0, minOf(16, input.size))
    return buf.getLong(0) xor buf.getLong(8)
}
```

C2 will often prove the `ByteBuffer` never escapes the method (escape analysis), then replace it with stack-allocated primitives (scalar replacement). Your benchmark will show near-zero allocation cost. But in production, if this same method is called through an interface with multiple implementations, the inliner gives up, EA fails, and you allocate for real. Run JMH with the GC profiler to see allocation rate:

```bash
./gradlew jmh -Pargs="-prof gc"
```

Look at `·gc.alloc.rate.norm` (bytes per op). If the benchmark shows 0 B/op but production shows 48 B/op, you've been fooled by EA.

**To actually see what the JIT did**, dump the compilation log:

```bash
java -XX:+UnlockDiagnosticVMOptions -XX:+PrintCompilation \
     -XX:+PrintInlining -XX:+LogCompilation -XX:LogFile=jit.log \
     -jar bench.jar
```

`PrintCompilation` shows each method as it gets compiled with a `3` or `4` suffix for C1/C2 tier. `PrintInlining` shows inlining decisions — look for "too big" or "callee is too large" or "not inlineable" on your hash function. If your strategy A isn't getting inlined and B is, that alone explains the gap.

For the report to your tech lead, the credible artifact is:

1. JMH output with 3 forks, 10+10 iterations, error bars shown.
2. GC profiler output showing allocation cost is the same or different.
3. A sentence like "after proper warmup, strategy B is 1.2x faster than A at our target input size, p<0.001" — if it even holds up.

And if it DOESN'T hold up, that's a better outcome than shipping a wrong recommendation.

```python
import queue
import threading

class ConnectionPool:
    def __init__(self, create_conn, max_size=10):
        self._create = create_conn
        self._pool = queue.Queue(maxsize=max_size)
        self._size = 0
        self._max = max_size
        self._lock = threading.Lock()

    def acquire(self, timeout=5.0):
        # Try to get an existing connection
        try:
            return self._pool.get_nowait()
        except queue.Empty:
            pass

        # Create a new one if under limit
        with self._lock:
            if self._size < self._max:
                self._size += 1
                return self._create()

        # Wait for one to be returned
        return self._pool.get(timeout=timeout)

    def release(self, conn):
        try:
            self._pool.put_nowait(conn)
        except queue.Full:
            conn.close()  # Pool is full, discard
            with self._lock:
                self._size -= 1

    def __enter__(self):
        self._conn = self.acquire()
        return self._conn

    def __exit__(self, *exc):
        self.release(self._conn)

# Usage
pool = ConnectionPool(lambda: sqlite3.connect('app.db'), max_size=5)

with pool as conn:
    conn.execute('SELECT 1')
```

This is the PATCH problem and it has a canonical solution in gRPC: `google.protobuf.FieldMask`. Combined with `optional` scalars, you get clean partial updates.

**Why proto3 makes it fuzzy by default.** An `int32 amount = 3;` is zero when unset and zero when explicitly set to zero. Without more information, the server receiving an `UpdateOrderRequest { amount: 0 }` cannot tell: did the client want to clear amount, or just omit it?

**Two pieces to the fix: `optional` (distinguishes unset from zero) and `FieldMask` (says which fields the client meant to update).**

*Schema:*

```protobuf
import "google/protobuf/field_mask.proto";

service OrderService {
  rpc UpdateOrder(UpdateOrderRequest) returns (Order);
}

message UpdateOrderRequest {
  // The resource with updated values. Only fields listed in update_mask are read.
  Order order = 1;

  // Required. The list of fields to update. Use "*" for full replacement.
  google.protobuf.FieldMask update_mask = 2;
}

message Order {
  string id = 1;
  string customer_id = 2;
  optional int64 amount_cents = 3;           // optional => has_amount_cents() works
  optional string notes = 4;                 // can distinguish "" from unset
  OrderStatus status = 5;
  google.protobuf.Timestamp shipped_at = 6;  // messages are nullable by default
}
```

*Client sends:*

```go
req := &pb.UpdateOrderRequest{
    Order: &pb.Order{
        Id:       "ord_8812",
        Notes:    proto.String(""),     // explicitly clear notes
        Status:   pb.OrderStatus_ORDER_STATUS_SHIPPED,
    },
    UpdateMask: &fieldmaskpb.FieldMask{
        Paths: []string{"notes", "status"},
    },
}
client.UpdateOrder(ctx, req)
```

This says: "Update exactly `notes` (clear it to empty string) and `status` (set to SHIPPED). Ignore everything else in the `Order` message, including `amount_cents`."

*Server applies:*

```go
func (s *orderServer) UpdateOrder(ctx context.Context, req *pb.UpdateOrderRequest) (*pb.Order, error) {
    if req.UpdateMask == nil || len(req.UpdateMask.Paths) == 0 {
        return nil, status.Error(codes.InvalidArgument, "update_mask required")
    }
    order, err := s.repo.Get(ctx, req.Order.Id)
    if err != nil {
        return nil, err
    }
    for _, path := range req.UpdateMask.Paths {
        switch path {
        case "notes":
            order.Notes = req.Order.Notes  // *string, nil-safe
        case "status":
            order.Status = req.Order.Status
        case "amount_cents":
            order.AmountCents = req.Order.AmountCents
        default:
            return nil, status.Errorf(codes.InvalidArgument,
                "unsupported field in update_mask: %s", path)
        }
    }
    return s.repo.Save(ctx, order)
}
```

Libraries like `fieldmaskutils` (Go) or Google's `FieldMaskUtil` (Java) will apply a mask generically via reflection, but for non-trivial services, hand-written field handling is clearer and lets you validate per-field (e.g., "you can't change `id` via PATCH, full stop").

**`optional` vs. wrapper types (`Int32Value` etc.).**

Before proto3.15, the idiomatic solution was `google.protobuf.Int32Value`, `StringValue`, `BoolValue` — wrapper messages around scalars. Message presence (`wrapper != nil`) gave you "is it set." These still work and you'll see them in older proto files, but `optional` is the modern choice and compiles to cleaner accessors in every language. The wrappers are still useful at the edges (JSON transcoding serializes them as `{"value": 42}` in older configurations, though most transcoders now handle `optional` correctly).

**Three rules for PATCH in gRPC.**

1. *Always require `update_mask`.* If the client sends no mask, reject with INVALID_ARGUMENT. Never infer "update every set field" — you'll overwrite fields the client didn't mean to touch. Google's own API guidelines enforce this.
2. *Validate mask paths.* Reject unknown or disallowed paths (e.g., `id`, `created_at`). A typo in the mask should fail loudly, not silently do nothing.
3. *Use `optional` on scalars the client might want to clear.* Non-optional scalars in proto3 can't be distinguished from zero, so "set this to zero" and "leave alone" collapse. With `optional`, `order.Notes = nil` is "unset" and `order.Notes = proto.String("")` is "explicitly empty."

**Full replacement: `update_mask: ["*"]`.** By convention, a mask of `*` means "replace the whole resource with what I sent; fields not in the request message are reset to defaults." This is PUT semantics. Decide at API-design time whether you want to support it — many teams only support mask-based PATCH to reduce footguns.

That's the pattern: `optional` + `FieldMask`. It's more code than "just send the diff," but it makes the intent explicit on the wire, which is exactly what you want for a long-lived contract between services.

Yes, this is the classic virtualization-meets-a11y problem. Native focus follows the DOM, but virtualization unmounts off-screen rows, so when you arrow past the viewport the next row doesn't exist yet and focus drops onto the body.

There are two viable patterns. Pick based on your needs:

**Pattern 1: Roving tabindex with imperative focus.** Keep focus on the actual row, scroll it into view, then re-focus after it mounts. This is what most tables (TanStack, react-aria) do.

```tsx
function VirtualTable({ rows }: { rows: Row[] }) {
  const [focusIndex, setFocusIndex] = useState(0);
  const listRef = useRef<FixedSizeList>(null);
  const rowRefs = useRef<Map<number, HTMLDivElement>>(new Map());

  function onKeyDown(e: React.KeyboardEvent) {
    let next = focusIndex;
    if (e.key === 'ArrowDown') next = Math.min(rows.length - 1, focusIndex + 1);
    else if (e.key === 'ArrowUp') next = Math.max(0, focusIndex - 1);
    else if (e.key === 'Home') next = 0;
    else if (e.key === 'End') next = rows.length - 1;
    else return;
    e.preventDefault();
    setFocusIndex(next);
    listRef.current?.scrollToItem(next, 'smart'); // ensures it gets rendered
  }

  // After scroll causes the row to mount, focus it.
  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    rowRefs.current.get(focusIndex)?.focus();
  }, [focusIndex]);

  const Row = ({ index, style }: ListChildComponentProps) => (
    <div
      ref={el => {
        if (el) rowRefs.current.set(index, el);
        else rowRefs.current.delete(index);
      }}
      role="row"
      tabIndex={index === focusIndex ? 0 : -1}
      style={style}
    >
      {/* cells */}
    </div>
  );

  return (
    <div role="grid" onKeyDown={onKeyDown}>
      <FixedSizeList
        ref={listRef}
        itemCount={rows.length}
        itemSize={40}
        height={600}
        width="100%"
        overscanCount={5}
      >
        {Row}
      </FixedSizeList>
    </div>
  );
}
```

Key points:
- `tabIndex={-1}` on non-focused rows, `0` on the focused one (roving tabindex).
- `scrollToItem('smart')` only scrolls if the row is out of view, which prevents jitter.
- `useLayoutEffect` (not `useEffect`) so focus moves before the browser paints — no flicker.
- The ref callback cleans up unmounted rows from the map, otherwise it leaks references to stale DOM nodes.
- `role="grid"` on the container is mandatory because virtualization breaks `<table>` semantics for screen readers.

**Pattern 2: aria-activedescendant.** Keep DOM focus on the container; just update the active descendant id.

```tsx
<div
  role="listbox"
  tabIndex={0}
  aria-activedescendant={`row-${focusIndex}`}
  onKeyDown={onKeyDown}
>
  {/* virtualized rows with id={`row-${index}`} */}
</div>
```

This sidesteps the unmount problem entirely (focus never has to chase the DOM), and it's what comboboxes use. The downside: it's `role="listbox"` semantics, not `role="grid"`, so it's a poor fit for multi-column tables where users navigate cells with arrow keys.

For your table, go with pattern 1. Test it with VoiceOver (Cmd+F5 on macOS) — you should hear each row announced as you arrow through, and PageDown/Home/End should all work.

